# Sibling sources staged into the build context, plus the pins in the "LibreLane" and
# "OpenROAD, for the Nix shell" sections. The file is sourced as shell under set -euo pipefail
# (by stage-sources.sh, and by nix/cli-env.sh in a fresh bash to read one value), so it must
# source cleanly. flake.nix, nix/check-build-pins.sh and nix/check-openroad-pin.sh also match
# some assignments as whole lines and refuse unless exactly one line matches; the two checks
# also refuse a value the shell reads differently from the literal. So keep every assignment
# on one line, a plain literal quoted as it is now, with nothing after it.
#
# adk-tools is a lockfile. Every tool it ships is normally a submodule under
# tools/, pinned by sha, and build.sh writes those shas into manifest.json.
# The repositories in the "Staged repositories" section cannot enter that way
# today, and the test is whether a second person can OBTAIN THE PINNED COMMIT,
# not whether the repository is public. Two different things fail it:
#
#   - the remote is personal or private, or the checkout has no remote at all,
#     so there is no URL a second person can use (seven of the eight entries);
#   - the remote exists but does not have that commit, because it has not been
#     pushed there.
#
# The eighth entry, chiplet-spec, passes the test now (its pinned commit is on its
# remote) and stays staged until it moves to a submodule; see the chiplet-spec entry.
#
# Either way a submodule URL would point at something a second person cannot
# resolve, which is worse than no pin at all.
#
# So they are STAGED instead: stage-sources.sh, which build.sh runs before the image
# build, fetches the pinned commit from a local sibling checkout into a new, shallow
# repository under staging/ and checks it out; it never copies the working tree. The
# difference is the whole point. A fetch takes a commit, so uncommitted edits,
# untracked .venv directories and __pycache__ cannot ride along, and what lands in
# the image is exactly what the recorded sha says. The staged tree keeps its .git,
# which packaging-flow and chiplet-flow need to derive each package's identity;
# stage-sources.sh has the full reason. build.sh reads each staged tree's HEAD back
# and records it in manifest.json under its own "staged" key, beside the submodule
# shas.
#
# What this costs: the image is not rebuildable from this repository alone.
# Rebuilding it requires these commits to exist in a local checkout. That is a
# real reduction in provenance and it was an explicit decision, not an
# oversight. It is still strictly better than copying a working tree, where
# "what is in the image" has no name at all.
#
# Each STAGE_*_PATH is relative to this repository's parent directory, or to
# STAGE_SIBLINGS when that is set.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Staged repositories
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Assembly-stage LVS engine: distribution `assembly-lvs` 0.8.0, console script `assembly-lvs`. In
# the image, the `assembly-lvs` on PATH is bin/assembly-lvs, which execs the venv's script. It
# reads an assembly-lvs.resolved/v2, /v3, /v4 or /v5 document and answers a v2 input with an
# assembly-lvs.report/v3 report, a v3 input with a report/v4, a v4 input with a report/v5 and a
# v5 input with a report/v6. v3 is
# v2 plus an optional per-die footprint policy (an unselected frame on the pad layer, admitted
# only if proved isolated), and report/v4 adds that isolation. v4 is v3 plus `purpose: fill` on a
# layer mapping: fill is merged with the drawn metal of its layer first, then a carrier polygon
# made only of declared fill is removed when it provably cannot join a net (dies are never
# pruned), and report/v5 records what was removed per carrier and layer. For a v2 or v3 input
# 0.6.0 changes only engine.version and the run id against 0.5.0; the nine states and their exit
# codes are 0.5.0's. The staged packaging-flow writes a v4 input for every run with a carrier,
# the carrier's FILL mappings declared fill, so it needs 0.6.0. packaging-flow's assembly-LVS
# gate runs `assembly-lvs` from PATH by default (CHIPLET_FLOW_ASSEMBLY_LVS overrides it) and
# accepts only the report schema that pairs with its input and this version's state table, so
# this entry moves with the packaging-flow entry.
#
# It requires klayout>=0.30,<0.31, PyYAML>=6,<7 and z3-solver>=4.15,<4.16, and uses KLayout
# through the Python module (klayout.db), not a klayout binary. In the image, the Dockerfile's
# KLAYOUT_PIP (0.30.5) and PYYAML_PIP (6.0.3) are in range; z3-solver has no pin there, so pip
# resolves it within that range when it installs the staged trees. The Nix shell installs this
# tree with --no-deps and then runs pip check, so the KLayout and z3 it takes from Nix must meet
# the same ranges (flake.nix adds the z3-solver metadata that check reads). KLayout 0.31 is
# outside the range: moving KLAYOUT_PIP or the Nix KLayout to 0.31 needs an assembly-lvs
# revision that admits it, in the same change.
#
# The repository's history has been started again twice, each time from one commit whose tree is
# the old history's last commit. The first brought GPL-2.0-or-later, its docs pages and an
# allowlist image context, with setuptools>=77 as the build backend, which the wheelhouse's 84.0.0
# meets. Five commits on it then make the fill checks cheaper (a component's
# translation taken once per region, the geometric fill checks repeated only when their inputs
# change) and write test layouts without GDS timestamps; the reports and the state table do not
# change. Version 0.7.0 then also writes a KLayout LVS database, <report stem>.lvsdb, beside a
# report written to a file; the arguments are the same, and the report changes only in
# engine.version and the run id. The second start holds the tree of
# that 0.7.0 commit plus three commits: pyproject.toml drops the tier runner's table, the reference
# reproduction record is regenerated for 0.7.0 and the test fixtures are renamed. Under src/ only a
# comment moves, so the version (0.7.0), the arguments and the reports are 0.7.0's.
#
# An earlier move adds four commits: the repository licence moves to GPL-3.0-or-later, the
# README becomes a short page for a newcomer, the docs say when verify writes an LVS database and
# tabulate the format versions, and CI gates on the host tier, generated by the CI runner from the
# tier row. Under src/ only the SPDX lines move, so the version (0.7.0), the arguments and the
# reports are 0.7.0's.
#
# An earlier move adds one CI commit: the .github/ci/read_pins.py reader, regenerated by the CI
# runner, which can also name the image's IHP-Open-PDK and gf180mcu commits (SG13G2_REF,
# GF180MCU_REF). No package or test file changes.
#
# This ref (27e8508, 0.8.0) adds three commits on the previous pin: resolved/v5 is v4 plus a declared pin
# tie, a group of pads a die declares as one pin (evidence kind declared_component_contract, an
# assembly-lvs.component-certificate/v3 naming the declaration it was read from). A declared tie
# joins nothing: each pad is still judged as its own terminal, and report/v6 lists the declared
# groups and says they are not verified. v2, v3 and v4 inputs keep their report versions with
# unchanged content apart from engine.version and the run id, so the staged packaging-flow's v4
# runs read as before. The dependency ranges do not move.
STAGE_ASSEMBLY_LVS_PATH=assembly-lvs
# This ref (27e8508) is assembly-lvs dev after the v2026.11 release, on the previous pin
# 27e8508: one commit, the v2026.11 release bytes (its public references); no code moves.
STAGE_ASSEMBLY_LVS_REF=27e850825e11035871a8023d94573b502f32af87

# 2.5D place/route engine, distribution interposer-pnr 0.5.0. Console script `interposer-pnr`
# (interposer_pnr.cli:main), a headless subcommand CLI, not a GUI: place, place-check, bumps,
# io-pads, route, pdn, check-mesh, check-requirements, export, board-revision, report, signoff.
# In the image, the `interposer-pnr` on PATH is bin/interposer-pnr, which execs the venv's script. Its two requirements, jsonschema>=4.18
# and pyyaml>=6.0, are met by the venv pins, and pip check runs after the install. klayout appears
# only in its test extra, which the image does not install, so it puts no constraint on KLAYOUT_PIP.
# The adk-tools Nix shell (nix/cli-env.sh) installs it editable with --no-deps and then runs pip
# check, so flake.nix's Python packages must meet the same two requirements.
#
# Its cross-repo inputs (the ADK's rule parameters, the interconnect method manifest) come from
# an option or the environment, never from the checkout layout; with neither it refuses (exit 5)
# rather than use its built-in numbers, unless --allow-builtin-rules opts in. The image sets
# ADK_ROOT and INTERCONNECT_PDK_ROOT for them. A synthesized board also reads the interposer
# PDK's libs.tech/intm4tm2_stack.json (--interposer-pdk-root, else INTERPOSER_PDK_ROOT, which
# the image sets) for its body thickness and stackup; that key has no built-in, so synthesis
# refuses without it even under --allow-builtin-rules. It reads the key at schema_version 1.1,
# which adds beol.passivation, a block it does not read; a 1.0 key still reads. The
# tools/OpenIntM4TM2 gitlink carries the key, so this pin and that gitlink move together. It
# checks each file's declared version with chiplet_format_io.check_contract_version when that
# imports, which in the image is the staged chiplet-spec reader (see the chiplet-spec entry), and
# with an identical local rule otherwise.
#
# OpenROAD. The image carries the build NIX_OPENROAD_OUTPATH names in its own /nix/store and sets
# OPENROAD in its ENV (an image built before that has none, and run.sh supplies one at run time):
# interposer-pnr takes $OPENROAD first (a value that does not resolve is an error, not a fallback),
# then `openroad` on PATH, and with neither `route` exits 2 and writes nothing. `adk-tools` reports
# whether $OPENROAD or PATH has one. The build is not interchangeable. interposer-pnr documents its
# routing as proven on one OpenROAD build, the one its tools_manifest.json names as the routing
# reference (probed through its own lookup, $OPENROAD then PATH, under the live_openroad marker;
# drift there warns, never fails). Its docs record that on ad9e7248, the build
# openroad/openroad.pin pins for the 3Dblox sidecar image, rdl_route over its own fixture spins at
# 100% CPU for over 13 minutes where that build finishes in seconds; tools_manifest.json keeps that
# build for 3Dblox work only. The Nix shell exports OPENROAD as dcf36133, the build LibreLane
# packages (see the "OpenROAD, for the Nix shell" section). dcf36133's detailed_route aborts on
# -bottom/-top_routing_layer (DRT-0509/0510); this rev's routing Tcl drops those flags only when
# detailed_route's own proc body rejects both, and any probe error keeps them, so the routing
# reference still gets them. This rev's Tcl also corrects dcf36133's rdl_route, which drops every
# net given by name. Moving the routing reference to dcf36133 is interposer-pnr's work, not
# adk-tools'.
#
# Why this rev. It routes each die endpoint against the drawn top-metal landing rather than the
# pillar body, derived per method from the interconnect manifest's fab_params as the
# passivation opening plus twice the TopMetal2 enclosure. In the manifest at the image's
# IHP-Interconnect-IntM4TM2 gitlink that is 50 um against a 44 um body for cupillar_opt1;
# landing minus body is 6 um for all three cupillar options, 0 for sbump_sac305 and 10 for
# vendorx_microbump. A router clearing from the body believes it has half that difference more
# room per side than exists (3 um on a cupillar), and the shortfall is invisible to any check
# that does not know the landing. A method without a complete fab_params pair falls back to the
# body, and the route says so in its notes.
#
# It also carries what the flow around it uses: the KiCad round trip (export with --chiplet and
# --bumps records what was sent in its --report, and signoff --sign --report checks that a board
# edited in KiCad still carries that copper; for a host that signs on someone's behalf,
# --expect-sha256 refuses a board other than the one whose fingerprint was confirmed and
# --refuse-existing refuses when anything is already at --ack, both with exit 3 before anything is
# written, and without them the record bytes are unchanged); die footprints from --pins, whose
# electrical pads are the carrier landing circles, with --layout binding each die's GDS (with
# --board-relative-layout a relative one is read from the board's folder and written as given, so
# two runs of one design write the same board); carrier io_pad footprints; the board-revision
# subcommand; a pad exit keepout (routability.pad_exit_um, 7.0 um from rule Pad.fR in both IntM4TM2
# descriptors, written as DEF blockages per routed I/O pad on multi-layer routes); and a
# routing-only access view for circular pads (pin_access.py) that changes no exported geometry.
# Each die footprint carries a CHIPLET_PAD_DOMAINS record (format chiplet_kicad.pad_domains,
# version 1), the one the image's KiCad export plugin validates.
#
# packaging-flow runs it as `python -m interposer_pnr` from its own interpreter (the image's
# venv, unless CHIPLET_FLOW_ENGINE_PYTHON names another) and never imports it. It passes
# --adk-root and --interconnect-pdk-root on every call, uses export --pins/--layout (a layout
# inside the run folder with --board-relative-layout, which an older rev rejects as an unknown
# option) and board-revision, reads exits 5 and 6 as a source it could not use or a source
# version it must not read, and accepts the export report's round_trip record at versions 1 to 5
# (3 and 5 follow a sheet, 4 and 5 carry reserved copper); a change to any of these moves with the
# packaging-flow entry. The signoff acknowledgment it writes is version 2 of
# interposer_pnr.signoff (a version 1 ack still reads, as unstated); the packaging-flow ref reads
# both versions through one loader. `signoff --check` writes its own record and signs nothing.
# The die flow (chiplet-flow) does not run it. In this repository only nix/capture_context.py
# imports it, to record its file and version. The verify stage checks that the staged tree reads
# back this commit as a user that does not own it, as for every staged tree, and its step 0c holds
# the tree's frozen copy of the interposer PDK's key file,
# tests/data/frozen/interposer_pdk/libs.tech/intm4tm2_stack.json, to the PDK this image carries
# (check_attach_plane.py --pdk-copy). interposer-pnr's own suite reports a frozen file that drifted
# as a skip, so a PDK pin that moves the file fails the build until interposer-pnr refreshes its
# copy. Nothing the verify stage runs imports interposer_pnr.
#
# The repository's history has been started again twice. A KiCad draft decides each of its rule files once, acts on one only while it is as
# the plan found it, writes it through a temporary file, and stops on an unreadable one (--force
# does not get past it); a template's clearance reaches KiCad through its .kicad_dru. The licence is
# GPL-3.0-or-later AND Apache-2.0, with the Apache-2.0 schemas and routing descriptors named in a
# REUSE.toml the wheel ships. The version is 0.5.0. Its own .chiplet reader (ir.py) accepts format
# 1.1, refuses a source document (a top-level system block) on the assembly read path and any
# assembly.units other than um, as the reference reader from 1.14.0 does; an inventory refusal names
# its footprints; and `signoff --sign` takes --channel terminal|form|script and writes ack version
# 3. Its suite reads each sibling through its own variable before the shared root and declares its
# T2 skips. A bare pytest starts no tool binary and no container, and tools_manifest.json reads the
# klayout and Python versions from this repository's ci/pin-set.json. Nothing the verify stage runs
# imports interposer_pnr.
#
# An earlier move adds seven commits: live routing inputs come from an explicit run
# directory, declared skip totals are accepted for optional run inputs, the frozen run provenance
# drops private directory labels, the repository licence moves to GPL-3.0-or-later, and the
# README becomes a short page with the detail under docs/. The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds eleven commits. The reader accepts format 1.3 and, as the
# reference reader from 1.16.0 does, refuses an assembly that carries a source's unplaced key. A
# new place command decides the dies a source leaves open, holding fixed dies and boxing them
# from their pin lists; with --keep-all it keeps every die and only checks them. The two IHP routing
# descriptors (schema 1.2) name the technology's fab layer set, fab.layer_set, a path under the
# interposer PDK root that the tools/OpenIntM4TM2 gitlink carries; packaging-flow's fab-file reads
# it, so this entry, that one and the gitlink move together. The lambdapdk descriptor stays at 1.1
# without one, so fab-file refuses that technology. CI gates on the host tier. The version stays
# 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 25 commits. export --form draws the board against the designer's KiCad
# sheet: die footprints take their symbol's identity and units, carrier pads their sheet EXT_PAD
# identity in a hidden IO_PAD_ID property, every pad a die joins is netted, and routed copper takes
# the sheet's net names. With --form the export report's round_trip is version 3 and states the way
# back (net_names, planner to sheet, and sheet_nets, the pads no routed net binds); a form board is
# checked against its sheet as KiCad's Update PCB from Schematic would, and the gate refuses
# jumpers. src/interposer_pnr/round_trip_names.py is the one reader of that record: gds_to_kicad
# and the plugin vendor it byte-identical, each beside a SOURCE file naming the commit it came from, so when the
# file changes the two gitlinks and this entry move together. chiplet-spec's reader (__init__.py
# and footprint_ids.py) is vendored under src/interposer_pnr/_vendor. The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds three commits, and a CI commit. signoff --report and
# board_revision check a round_trip 3 report's net names through read_round_trip_names, so a net
# map no export writes (one board net for two planner nets, at any version; at version 3, also a
# sheet net that is a routed net's board name) is refused as an unreadable receipt (usage exit),
# not reported as a source-net-map finding. round_trip_names.py is unchanged, so its vendored
# copies and their SOURCE files stay current. place --rotations names the turns a die may
# end at (of 0, 90, 180 and 270; all four by default): placed dies turn only by those, a kept die
# at another turn is not_legal rather than turned, and the placement records them. The CI commit is
# the .github/ci/read_pins.py reader, regenerated by the CI runner, which can also name the image's
# IHP-Open-PDK and gf180mcu commits (SG13G2_REF, GF180MCU_REF). The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds three commits. OpenROAD 02c5d66d9a lost the list variable in
# rdl_route's net-name loop, so every net given by name is appended to nothing and the command ends
# in PAD-0042; dcf36133, the build this image ships, has that body, so interposer-pnr's
# single-layer backend routed no net in the image. The rdl Tcl now reads the proc body first: a
# broken loop has the proc redefined with only that line corrected, either known correct spelling
# (the restored foreach or the later lmap) is left alone, and anything else stops before rdl_route
# and route() refuses. The verify stage's step 0g runs its three live_openroad route cases on
# $OPENROAD (ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt), so this entry and that list move together. No vendored file
# moves. The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 24 commits. It reads format 1.4 and vendors chiplet_format_io
# 1.18.0 from chiplet-spec (its SOURCE names the commit), and the loader's accepted minor
# is held to the vendored reader's, so this entry moves with STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_REF. A
# net_requirements v1 file states per-net trace widths, checked before a route and reported per
# net after it. Each routing layer's width-dependent spacing is stated in the descriptor and the
# tech LEF (no longer an NDR), and interposer-pnr measures every routed gap between nets with its
# own spacing check, keeping every finding of a refused route. A routed DEF's TAPER run heads are
# read, and the DIEAREA sits on the manufacturing grid. Every pad of a rail pin is an endpoint (V18
# step 3): a rail group is read as one assignment at every round_trip version, board_revision folds
# a repeated rail connection per net, and a rail group on rdl, or a repeat on another layer, is
# refused before routing. The three route cases in ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt are unchanged. No
# pyproject dependency moves. The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds five commits. A route names each system net on every backend:
# traces.json and route.json's unrouted[].net carry the design's net name whether rdl decomposed
# the nets or grt+drt routed them whole (unrouted[].subnet keeps the DEF name), through one
# DEF-to-system map, and a routed net missing from that map is refused. export writes net names
# verbatim, on a new board and onto a designer's (--board), and refuses before writing a name
# outside letters, digits, '_', '.' and a bracketed digit index after the first character. That
# is gds_to_kicad's shape without its '+' and '-', which the board side has not measured, so a
# net gds_to_kicad draws can still be refused here. A --form export carries KiCad's sheet names
# and is not held to it. The vendored chiplet_format_io moves to 1.19.0 from chiplet-spec
# (its SOURCE names the commit; only __version__ changes), so this entry moves with
# STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_REF. round_trip_names.py does not change, so its vendored copies and their
# SOURCE files stay current. The three route cases in ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt are
# unchanged. No pyproject dependency moves. The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 18 commits. A route takes reserved copper (--reserved stripes
# and tiled via arrays, planned against the design and reported per owner), and round_trip versions
# 4 and 5 record it. read_round_trip_names reads the reserved block at every version, so
# round_trip_names.py moves and its vendored copies move with this entry: the
# tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin and tools/gds_to_kicad gitlinks carry it byte-equal, their SOURCE
# files naming that commit, and packaging-flow's entry accepts versions 4 and 5. check-requirements
# prints route's requirement refusals for a net_requirements file before any route, reading no
# design and running no OpenROAD; packaging-flow's entry runs it, so this entry and that one move
# together. It also freezes the intm4tm2 0.2.0 KiCad rule templates (rules TM2_bR and Slt_c) as
# test data, byte-equal to the interposer PDK's interposer_template files (c07d01f's, which the
# tools/OpenIntM4TM2 gitlink's df4485d keeps), which export copies beside the board unread; a
# change to that pair moves this entry and that gitlink together. Step 0c's
# check_kicad_templates.py holds the pair, and packaging-flow's fixture copy, to the gitlink's
# bytes. That move reads format 1.5 and vendors chiplet_format_io 1.20.0 from
# chiplet-spec (its SOURCE names the commit; footprint_ids.py does not change), so this
# entry moves with STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_REF.
# The three route cases in ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt are unchanged. No pyproject dependency moves.
# The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds six commits. A pdn stage plans a rail mesh from its spec and
# the prepared route, draws it with pdngen, reads every drawn shape back, refuses copper the spec
# did not ask for or a rail that falls apart, and writes a reserved file owned by pdn, which route
# takes like any reserved geometry. Its tests run a stand-in openroad; no image step runs pdngen
# yet, so pdn is not proven live here. route's code is reworked to share with pdn (rdl_route runs
# through openroad.run_script, the endpoint LEF and the die area move into shared helpers, and
# plan_reserved accepts a stripe width float-close to its bounds), but its arguments are one set
# with pdn's and unchanged, and check-requirements keeps its own. No vendored file,
# round_trip_names.py or tests/test_router.py moves, so the three route cases in
# ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt are unchanged and step 0g runs them on the reworked route. packaging-flow
# 56affc1 runs no pdn, and no template or reader copy moves, so packaging-flow, chiplet-spec and
# the gitlinks stay. No pyproject dependency moves. The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 19 commits. A net_requirements row on a reserved net is held to
# the reserved copper's cross-section, the copper a cut meets summed per direction at the narrowest
# cut, and route reports it as met_by_reserved instead of drawing a rule for it. A new check-mesh
# subcommand refuses a pdn mesh spec (interposer_pnr.mesh_spec v1, or v2 with a span per stripe)
# before any build, against the tech's rules, declared rails (interposer_pnr.rails v1), the voltage
# domain, the keep-in and that cross-section rule, and writes interposer_pnr.mesh_check v1;
# check-requirements --judged-by leaves to it only the rows it judged. round_trip moves to version
# 6, a self-describing record whose features list names the blocks it holds (pad_domains, sheet,
# reserved, widths). Given route's report through the new --route-report, export writes it with a
# widths block: each required net's per-layer width and the taper boxes route judged it by, or the
# cross-section of a met_by_reserved net. The draft's .kicad_dru is no longer the PDK template
# copied unread: its bytes are the PDK's interposer_template.kicad_dru as an unchanged prefix
# followed by one NetName track_width rule per widened net and layer (the pin layer and
# cross-section nets left out), an early warning in KiCad only. signoff refuses a board whose copper
# on a required net is narrower than the record states, outside route's own taper boxes, as one more
# reason line; the signoff_check record stays at version 1. round_trip_names.py moves (blob
# be50250), so its vendored copies move with this entry: the tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin and
# tools/gds_to_kicad gitlinks carry it byte-equal with SOURCE files naming that commit, and
# packaging-flow's entry vendors it too and passes --route-report, which only this ref takes. The
# vendored chiplet_format_io moves to 1.21.0 from chiplet-spec (its SOURCE names the
# commit; only __version__ changes, footprint_ids.py does not), so this entry moves with
# STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_REF. The frozen interposer PDK key file and KiCad rule pair do not move; the
# tools/OpenIntM4TM2 gitlink's df4485d keeps c07d01f's bytes for both. The image runs neither pdn
# nor check-mesh on a live OpenROAD; tests/test_ndr_known_limit.py holds detailed_route's spacing
# miss under a non-default rule as a strict xfail that no image step runs. tests/test_router.py does
# not change, so the three route cases in ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt are unchanged. No pyproject
# dependency moves. The version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 14 commits. A routing-tech descriptor that breaks its own
# schema version is refused at load (DescriptorRefused, through one check_descriptor gate the
# library loader uses too), and other routed metals keep off an I/O pad, not only the pin layer
# (the schema's pad_exit_um text says so; no schema version moves). pdn takes the pad footprint
# blockages out of its run, keeps pin-layer stripes off every pad, its own rail's too, and keeps
# the clearance pdngen keeps beside pads and pad-exit keep-outs. A new pdn-plan subcommand proposes
# a mesh spec from the floorplan's pads, reads it back with the mesh check, needs no OpenROAD and
# writes a report with stable refusal ids. A test runs pdn through the real openroad on the two-die
# fixture; it skips without one, and ci-runner's T2 entry interposer-pnr-openroad runs it on the
# image. The frozen KiCad rule pair moves to intm4tm2 0.3.1 (TM2.bR on a pad becomes a
# warning), byte-equal to the interposer PDK's interposer_template files at the tools/OpenIntM4TM2
# gitlink set in the same commit, so this entry and that gitlink move together and step 0c's
# check_kicad_templates.py holds them. No vendored file, round_trip_names.py or
# tests/test_router.py moves, so the three route cases in ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt are unchanged and
# the vendored chiplet_format_io stays at 1.21.0. No pyproject dependency moves. The version stays
# 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 82 commits. A power-check stage judges each rail's IR drop
# and EM through OpenROAD's psm, with its own report, refusal ids and exit codes; escape-capacity
# measures how many pillar rows a die edge escapes; a draft board is paired with and checked
# against the draft sheet of the same design; the router refuses an openroad without the decoder
# methods its dump calls; pdn ends each mesh stripe at its last connection, and a pad joins a
# stripe only where one cut fits. The frozen KiCad rule pair moves to intm4tm2 0.4.1 (TM2.bR holds
# TopMetal2 copper only), byte-equal to the interposer PDK's interposer_template files at the
# tools/OpenIntM4TM2 gitlink set in the same commit, so this entry, packaging-flow's and that gitlink move
# together and step 0c's check_kicad_templates.py holds them. tests/test_router.py does not change,
# so the route case in ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt is unchanged. No pyproject dependency moves. The
# version stays 0.5.0.
#
# An earlier move adds one commit: the bump obstruction fixture's README no longer names a
# development commit. Prose only; nothing else moves.
#
# An earlier move adds five commits. Placement follows the ADK's rule_params.json 0.3: ASM_b
# moves from 50 to 200 um and the built-in fallback follows it. A rule file that is found but
# states no ASM_b or ASM_e, or states either out of range, is refused (exit 5) instead of being
# completed from built-in values; ASM_e 0 still reads as no area floor. ASM_g is not read (placement
# places no pads). tests/test_drc_parity.py reads rule_params.json from the ADK tree whose
# run_drc.py it runs, so it holds the tools/adk gitlink's value, and this entry and that gitlink
# move together. No vendored file, KiCad rule pair or tests/test_router.py moves, so the three
# route cases in ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt are unchanged. No pyproject dependency moves. The version stays
# 0.5.0.
STAGE_INTERPOSER_PNR_PATH=interposer-pnr
# An earlier move was interposer-pnr dev after the v2026.11 release, on top of the pin before it: the
# v2026.11 release bytes and the engine's version line after the 0.5.0 release (0.6.0.dev0), with the escape report naming the engine release,
# then the PDN work (mesh domains, plans on the real pads, pdn-plan refusing rails pdngen would not draw) and the
# detailed router check quoting drt's own check_drc when the spacing check refuses a route.
# No vendored file, KiCad rule pair, tests/test_router.py or pyproject dependency moves.
#
# An earlier move added two commits: plan.py stops importing names only its tests used, and the
# vendored reader moves to chiplet-spec's 1.22.0 (the vendored __init__.py changes only its
# release string).
#
# An earlier move (3e85398) added five commits, all under tests/: every fixture and test assembly that
# seats dies declares the interposer's attachment_surface_z, and the bump-obstruction and
# draft-path cases are elaborated again with it (only the chiplet digest moves in their bumps.json
# and sheet). No src/ file moves, so routing, placement and the version (0.5.0) hold. It moves with
# ci/check_attach_plane.py, which walks this tree (step 0c). The vendored reader's SOURCE names
# an earlier commit than STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_REF; the bytes are equal and nothing
# reads that commit, so it is aligned at the next reader move. No KiCad rule pair,
# tests/test_router.py or pyproject dependency moves, so ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt is unchanged.
#
# This ref (23e5b31) adds ten commits. It ships ihp_sg13g2_intm4tm2_rdl2_sbump, the rdl2
# descriptor with the die landing on the solder-bump UBM opening instead of the pillar's, and the
# carrier pad domains accept the anchors some shipped descriptor attaches through, instead of the
# pillar's alone. --tech also takes a carrier package manifest (libs.tech/adk/technology.json,
# with an optional #variant), reads the routing member it names and refuses bytes that differ from
# the stated sha256; a descriptor path or shipped name still reads as before. Its
# test_descriptor_package_copies holds every shipped IHP descriptor to a variant of the interposer
# package, and only the tools/OpenIntM4TM2 gitlink at d69aab4 has rdl2_sbump, so this entry and
# that gitlink move together. pyproject.toml changes only declared-skips: the two live two-die
# cases take an empty tiers list, since no runner tier selects them. No vendored file, KiCad rule
# pair or tests/test_router.py moves; ci/ipnr-route-tests.txt is unchanged and the version stays
# 0.5.0.
STAGE_INTERPOSER_PNR_REF=23e5b317f2e36a9b5384db7527faed0de00c7f08

# Orchestrator that chains die builds into the interposer flow from one spec: distribution
# packaging_flow, console script `packaging-flow` (packaging_flow.cli:main). In the image the
# `packaging-flow` on PATH is bin/packaging-flow, which execs the venv's script. Its commands are
# run, resume, status, map, validate, verify, lvs-bundle, lvs-verify, edit, compare-nets, views,
# place, review-copy, review-levels, tasks, fab-file and the verification commands verify-status,
# verify-run, verify-export, verify-open, verify-accept, verify-sign and sign-request, which writes
# a gate's request for verify-sign. verify-sign signs a run's status at its gate through the
# interposer-pnr in the flow's own interpreter, the venv's here, and asks its questions only at a
# terminal; verify-accept files a standing acceptance, also at a terminal. verify-run runs the
# co-simulation kind, assembly.sim at the gate, as a chiplet-cosim from-run process on a finished
# run, through the chiplet-cosim and cosim-python the tool table names, and imports nothing of
# chiplet-cosim. review-copy copies a run's board to a folder outside the run for KiCad, rewriting
# each relative GDS_FILE for that folder. review-levels lays a completed run's final files out in a
# folder beside it, one subfolder per level, as copies (the board, its schematic and both .chiplet
# files, their relative paths re-anchored) and relative links, and writes levels.txt; a second call
# writes nothing and it never deletes. Both read and write files only.
#
# It builds each die as a separate `chiplet-die-flow build-die` process: the die-flow command a run
# is given, else the `chiplet-die-flow` it finds on PATH (see the chiplet-flow entry), and a run
# that builds no die never starts it. So the image and the Nix CLI shell both put the two programs
# on PATH: the image through bin/, the shell through its venv's bin/. It runs the interposer-pnr
# engine as `python -m interposer_pnr` under the interpreter running the flow (or
# $CHIPLET_FLOW_ENGINE_PYTHON) and never imports it, which is why the image installs both into one
# venv (see the interposer-pnr entry for what the flow relies on from the engine). It runs
# assembly-lvs as a recorded stage, and its tail dispatches chiplet-export, adk-verify-carrier-drc
# and adk-drc, all on the image's PATH. Its exit codes are 0 to 11 and 130, and host-verify.sh,
# which runs `status` or `verify` in a pinned image over read-only mounts and passes the exit code
# through, keeps its own codes (64 to 75) clear of that table. verify-status, verify-run and
# verify-sign add 12 to 16, which status and verify never exit. Both read commands write nothing
# into the run on any path, which is what the read-only mounts need.
#
# Installed editable from the staged clone, and it has to be (see the Dockerfile's install step):
# it records each dispatched package's identity with `git rev-parse HEAD` on the directory
# find_spec resolves to, and `packaging-flow verify` refuses a full-tier run whose identity reads
# unavailable. For the same reason the Dockerfile marks every staged tree a git safe.directory,
# so a container run under a user that does not own the trees still reads each commit. The flow
# also reads its terminal profiles from profiles/ at the repository root, outside the package.
# Package data is klayout_scripts/*.py (scripts it runs as KLayout or klayout-module
# subprocesses, never imports). No identity leaf needs Docker: provenance.image_digest() is
# defined but no identity path calls it, so verify in a container without a Docker CLI does not
# refuse on that account.
#
# Declared runtime deps are pyyaml>=6.0, chiplet-system-description and chiplet-format-io>=1.15.0;
# the chiplet-full extra adds the reader's strict extra at the same floor. The reader floor is a
# plain dependency, so pip check reads it: a chiplet-spec pin below 1.15.0 fails the image's install
# step (chiplet-cosim has the same floor, the die flow 1.12.0). 1.12.0 maps ihp-sg13cmos5l to
# sg13cmos5l, which the admission verdict for such a contract rests on (this ref declares sg13cmos5l
# with no compose window, and says why), and holds READ_KINDS, which the die flow door imports.
# 1.10.0 keys the KLayout check options (DRC_THREADS, DRC_OPTIONS) by PDK, and this package reads
# them that way. 1.11.0 adds checks 1.1, whose one new run-check value is not_checked, and
# PORTS_NOT_CHECKED, the deck trees whose deck does not check the top-level port labels: admission
# reads a die whose ports run check is not_checked, under checks 1.1 and over such a deck tree, as
# met with a not_checked:ports gap, and any other not_checked still fails that run check.
# chiplet-flow writes that record (see its entry), so the two pins move together. Loading the CLI
# imports PyYAML, chiplet_system_description and chiplet_format_io (its ready_die_names). Its strict
# die-contract admission imports the top-level chiplet_format_io, which in the image is the
# installed chiplet-spec distribution, and accepts only strict capability revision 4, which that pin
# provides; the chiplet-spec entry and this one move together on that.
#
# The heavy tiers (smoke, full) build dies through chiplet-die-flow build-die, which needs LibreLane
# (see the chiplet-flow entry); this image has none, so no die is built in it and only dies that
# are admitted, not built, run here. `--tier smoke` truncates each build at synthesis by default
# and leaves no die GDS. `packaging-flow run --tier hermetic` cannot work from the CLI: the CLI
# injects no die flow and the hermetic tier refuses to default to one.
#
# Roots come from the environment (roots.py): each from its own variable, else
# $HCDP_ROOT/<sibling>, else unset and refused where it is needed; for the two engine roots a
# flow spec's adk_root or interconnect_pdk_root comes first. The image exports the ones the flow
# reads: ADK_ROOT and INTERCONNECT_PDK_ROOT for the engine legs, which get them on their argv
# (the latter is also set for every process the flow starts), INTERPOSER_PDK_ROOT for the
# carrier DRC deck, the terminal profiles' pad rules and, when the spec names no board template,
# the engine's --interposer-pdk-root (checked at S0 like the other two engine roots; its root and
# the stack key's digest go into the engine identity), GDS_TO_KICAD_ROOT for the pad-map
# extractor, and CHIPLET_KICAD_PLUGIN_ROOT for edit. An engine below the interposer-pnr
# pin rejects --interposer-pdk-root as an unknown option, so this pin, that one and the
# tools/OpenIntM4TM2 gitlink (which carries libs.tech/intm4tm2_stack.json) move together.
#
# packaging_flow.technologies declares every technology the flow's specs build from, in its
# TECHNOLOGIES tuple (INTM4TM2, SG13G2, GF180MCU), each naming a KLayout layer sheet and a stackup
# as ${VAR} paths; a spec names the ones its system uses from there. Building them needs
# Technology.layer_properties and .stackup, which the system description in the chiplet-spec entry
# has, so the two move together. Only Chiplet Studio opens those paths; nothing in this package or
# the die flow does. The verify stage imports that module and checks every path against Studio's own
# path table and the files the image ships (ci/check_tech_files.py). That check reads a sheet's
# layer numbers itself; a Studio older than the layer-name reading its pinned commit carries reads a
# source that carries a layer name (the gf180mcu sheet's) as 0/0, which the check does not see, so
# the tools/chiplet-studio gitlink moves with this pin as well.
#
# The drc row passes the assembly deck --run_dir (its own log folder in the run), which run_drc.py
# at the tools/adk gitlink takes. A completed run writes out/deliverables.json, a reading aid no
# stage reads, now at version 2 (each entry says what kind of file it is, and the exporter's .hyp
# is a gds output); status --json and verify --json carry the same pick under "deliverables",
# through frozen version 2 views, and keep their own versions.
#
# The examples seat their dies on the interposer PDK's attach plane: packaging_flow.attach_plane
# (examples/attach_plane.py re-exports it) reads beol.attachment_surface_z_um from
# libs.tech/intm4tm2_stack.json under the interposer PDK root (INTERPOSER_PDK_ROOT in the image)
# when a spec builds, not when it is imported, and adds each method's connection stack in decimal; a
# PDK without the key refuses the spec. The suite reads a byte copy of that file,
# tests/fixtures/interposer_pdk/libs.tech/intm4tm2_stack.json, with a PROVENANCE.md naming the PDK
# commit and sha256, and the verify stage's step 0c holds that copy to the PDK this image carries
# (check_attach_plane.py --pdk-copy). Examples 1, 5 and 8 (two_die_ready, two_die_wire_bond,
# two_die_echo_shifted_gf180) run their co-simulation case on the image's die views: their pages
# pass chiplet-cosim from-run --view-store "$ADK_DIE_VIEWS", which the image sets to its die views
# folder (see the Dockerfile).
#
# Unless the spec supplies a board template or one of its builds is truncated (the smoke tier), a
# run must state both ADK adapter ids (interposer_adapter, interconnect_adapter); the flow picks
# neither. Before any stage it also reads die_processes from manifest/interconnect_methods.json
# under the interconnect root and refuses a placed die its attachment method does not admit (a
# method that states none admits no die), so the tools/IHP-Interconnect-IntM4TM2 gitlink must
# state them for every method a run uses.
#
# The assembly-lvs engine is the one the command names (run --assembly-lvs-executable, lvs-verify
# --executable), else $CHIPLET_FLOW_ASSEMBLY_LVS, else `assembly-lvs` on PATH. The flow writes it
# an assembly-lvs.resolved/v4 input for every run with a carrier: each routing layer's FILL
# mapping of the carrier (four on intm4tm2) carries purpose fill, and a die whose technology
# declares a footprint policy (gf180mcu does) keeps it as in v3. It accepts only the report
# schema that pairs with the input, report/v5 for v4, whose state and exit code are in
# assembly-lvs 0.6.0's nine-state table, and reconciles the report's fill_pruning against the
# declared fill in both directions. An engine older than 0.6.0 answers a v4 input with a
# report/v3, which the flow refuses; the two entries move together. Runs and lvs-bundles recorded
# before v4 still verify against their v2 or v3 input; nothing new is written that way.
#
# The tail's gds row runs chiplet-export with --allow-drc-fail. At the tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin
# gitlink chiplet-export exits 3 when it wrote the files but the assembly DRC did not pass, and
# the flag turns that 3 into 0 and says so on the last line; the flow never reads that deck run,
# its drc row carries the verdict with counts. A plugin older than the flag refuses it as a usage
# error, so this pin and the gitlink move together.
#
# The same row passes --system-chiplet with the System .chiplet the run elaborated, from which the
# exporter takes each component's technology and the catalog entries those ids name; the board
# carries none. A plugin older than that flag refuses it as a usage error too.
#
# A die composed from an abstract has its own layout checked at admission: its admit row runs
# klayout_scripts/observe_frame.py under the pad-map interpreter and refuses unless the layout's
# unit, top cell and box are the contract's, in whole database units. The gds row then passes
# --die-layouts with a sidecar (out/die_layouts.json) naming each such layout, so the exported
# .chiplet names it, with assembly_view metadata pointing at the abstract; the complete GDS, the
# board, the .hyp and export.json do not move. The tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin gitlink takes
# --die-layouts, and a plugin older than the flag refuses it as a usage error, so this pin and
# the gitlink move together.
#
# A run with a terminal profile also finishes the carrier. Its gds row adds --seal-ring,
# --insert-metal-fill and --fill-mode closure, reads the seal-ring and fill-engine lines
# chiplet-export relays behind its "  | " prefix, and holds the fill engine to
# libs.tech/klayout/python/fill_closure.py under INTERPOSER_PDK_ROOT, the one root the exporter
# also takes it from when that variable is set. Its package-drc row then runs that PDK's
# libs.tech/klayout/tech/drc/run_drc.py over the finished carrier (the sealring deck with
# --require_sealring, and the density deck, at 4 threads), and verify's level 4 reads both rows.
# The tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin gitlink has the flags and prints those lines and the
# tools/OpenIntM4TM2 gitlink carries both files, so this pin and the two gitlinks move together.
#
# The schematic row runs chiplet_to_kicad_sch.py from the gds_to_kicad tree GDS_TO_KICAD_ROOT
# names, under the flow's own interpreter. The row never gates, and a tree without the script reads
# not_run. The tools/gds_to_kicad gitlink carries it, so this pin and that gitlink move together.
#
# edit writes a run's board or system sheet edits back into the system.chiplet the run read. A
# board edit runs the plugin's plugins/chiplet_export/board_snapshot_cli.py (under
# CHIPLET_KICAD_PLUGIN_ROOT, else --observer) with an interpreter that has pcbnew, reconciles
# through the interposer-pnr engine's board-revision command, and lifts the edits with
# chiplet_system_description.source.edit. A sheet edit (edit --sheet) reads a sheet KiCad saved,
# drawn from a blank page or from the handout chiplet_to_kicad_sch.py --handout writes, through
# kicad-cli and the gds_to_kicad tree's kicad_sch_to_connectivity.py; compare-nets holds a file's
# nets to a reference's with chiplet_system_description.source.compare. pip check sees none of
# that, and the description dependency carries no version floor, so this pin, the chiplet-spec and
# interposer-pnr entries and the tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin and tools/gds_to_kicad gitlinks move
# together.
#
# The run manifest keeps the format name chiplet_flow.manifest and is at version 5. Version 3's
# build rows stay: each die's build row names the build.json build-die wrote, the die-view row is
# gone (the view is build-die's), and identity.tools records this package as `flow` and what
# builds the dies as `die_flow`. Version 4 adds the co-simulation select answer and a gate that
# takes only the ack verify-sign writes; version 5, written for system.chiplet runs, keeps version
# 4's build rows. The reader takes legacy (neither key) and 1 to 5. verify --json is
# chiplet_flow.verify version 4 and status --json chiplet_flow.status version 1.
# No version since 2 has an assembly_lvs block: every run that composes the assembly derives its
# own assembly-LVS input and runs it, and `run` refuses --assembly-lvs-input. ci/resource_guard.py
# reads the same format and takes legacy and versions 1 to 5; at versions 3 to 5 it reads the
# LibreLane dispatch from each build row's facts.librelane_command, which this ref writes.
#
# Its test suite reaches the die flow through one door, the chiplet-flow checkout named by
# CHIPLET_FLOW_ROOT, at the commit this repository's ci/pin-set.json holds for chiplet-flow, which
# is STAGE_CHIPLET_FLOW_REF; at another commit, or with changes, every test fails on purpose. So
# the suite at this ref runs against whatever die flow the pin set names, and the two pins still
# move together, in the same commit as the regenerated pin set. The Nix CLI shell in
# evidence mode installs this ref from staging/ beside chiplet-flow (nix/cli-env.sh). The verify
# stage runs none of this package's tests; host-verify.sh runs its status and verify in a pinned
# image.
#
# This ref carries src/packaging_flow/verification/tracked_tree.py. nix/test_tracked_tree.py holds
# the port in nix/tool_identity.py to its tracked_tree_sha256, and ci/tracked-tree-tests.txt fails
# a run where it does not import, so this pin moves only to a commit that carries it.
#
# This ref's history starts at a new root (ci/pin-sources.json). Its signoff template leaves
# assembly.sim@gate out, so a signoff run does not read the image's co-sim readings table:
# packaging-flow's reader of that table (READINGS_SHA256) is older than the table chiplet-cosim
# writes, and when the reader follows it signoff lists the check again and this paragraph changes.
# Fast-iteration still lists the check, skipped. The ref reads the description as
# chiplet_system_description, runs chiplet-die-flow build-die, has validate and the map a run shows
# before and after it, the edit verb, verify version 4 and manifest version 5, typed review-levels
# refusals with a tighter automatic fill, interposer checklist item 2.3 (the DRC margins table, read
# from the carrier DRC's margins line), and run folders named by UTC stamp (2026-10-20_11-11-23Z),
# with the old form still read. It has edit --sheet and compare-nets (the edit paragraph above),
# draws each gate's layout as a picture beside the run (klayout_scripts/draw_view.py), and ships
# example 8's accepted.chiplet, a die admitted from a record with no DRC count. It is
# GPL-3.0-or-later, its editor extension under vscode/ too. Its dependencies name the description
# by its distribution name.
#
# This ref also ships kinds for the assembly DRC, the system DRC and the system terminal map, keyed
# on the adk-drc, intm4tm2-run-drc, adk-verify-carrier-drc, klayout, klayout-python and python rows
# of the tool table, and carries key.builder_keywords, which the kind contract check hands argv
# builders by. It records which setting bound the stage thread cap (tools.thread_cap_source). Its
# package data holds verification/review/*.json and the message catalog (messages/*.json, read by
# `python -m packaging_flow.messages`). It has the verb table (`packaging-flow tasks --vscode`,
# which writes the editor tasks bin/flow runs; its input commands are adk-packaging.input.*), map
# version 2 with a sign-request builder, and the editor extension under vscode/. A run of a
# system.chiplet given no --view-store takes the store ADK_DIE_VIEWS names, which the image sets,
# and records where it took it from (verification.view_store.from); a run named relative
# (runs/<stamp>, as bin/flow's tasks pass it) is made absolute before any stage reads it.
#
# An earlier move adds 67 commits. They relicense the package and the extension to
# GPL-3.0-or-later, take the die flow's test helpers again and restate their
# sources (the copied names did not change), open each check's log and
# margins from copies inside the run folder, show each die's declared checks with the flow's
# reading in the sign form, show external pad and bonding profile previews in the sign checklist,
# add an optional terminal console with coded build progress, and say design folder where the
# flow and the extension said example or project.
#
# An earlier move adds seven commits. The strict die-contract reader reads exactly
# capability revision 4, the one the chiplet-spec entry carries, and its test fails a set wider than
# the installed revision. The shifted gf180 pair declares its rail volts in its sources, and its
# three die contracts and build records are replaced by ones chiplet-flow emits from
# supply-volts files kept beside them; their layout and pin files are byte-identical. validate
# reports a voltage mismatch on a shared rail, and a note on a rail whose dies declare no
# supply_domains. The reader floor moves to 1.15.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 51 commits. The map pages read die build cards, show die
# project preparation and the recorded build, port and pad results at die gates, and persist
# coded build progress; Prepare launches run one at a time, and emitted die answers record
# structured port comparisons. A new fab-file command writes the carrier GDS that would go to the
# fab: it keeps only the layers of the set interposer-pnr's descriptor names (fab.layer_set, from
# interposer-pnr), holds the result to the manufacturing grid, and runs both carrier decks
# again as the flow builds them, writing nothing unless they give the run's verdict. CI gates on
# the host tier. The version stays 0.1.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 21 commits. The designer's KiCad sheet form is a run input, read once at
# the start and refused there if it cannot be used: when the form is unreadable, stale against its
# sheet or carries refusals; when the sheet's sym-lib-table is missing or does not name exactly the
# form's libraries by ${KIPRJMOD}; when the run draws no die footprint from the dies' own pins (a
# board template, or a die the tier truncates); or when the workdir is outside a run folder (the
# SheetFormRefused cases in src/packaging_flow/errors.py). A run writes the sheet and its board as
# one KiCad project in a review folder under review/sheet/. A new place command fills what a
# .chiplet leaves open (a die's position, the carrier's size, which edge each external pad takes),
# through edit's folder and dispatch. Every run hands chiplet-export the export report its gds row
# declares (--export-report); a plugin older than the flag refuses it as a usage error, so this pin
# and the tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin gitlink move together. The map pages keep pre-run actions in
# the Next bar and put Validate on pre-run cards. The version stays 0.1.0.
#
# An earlier move adds one CI commit: the .github/ci/read_pins.py reader, regenerated by the CI
# runner, which can also name the image's IHP-Open-PDK and gf180mcu commits (SG13G2_REF,
# GF180MCU_REF). No package or test file changes. The version stays 0.1.0.
#
# An earlier move adds 19 commits. A classic sheet is one a designer draws against
# the handout gds_to_kicad's chiplet_to_kicad_sch.py --handout writes. edit --sheet reads it as
# classic when chiplet_system.kicad_sym and the .chiplet's own <stem>.kicad_pro sit beside the
# .chiplet, and then refuses the edit unless the die library, chiplet_dies.kicad_sym, is there too;
# gds_to_kicad's kicad_sch_to_connectivity.py reads it with --classic and both libraries. A classic
# read from the .kicad_sch, rather than from a form given directly, keeps <stem>.connectivity.json
# beside the .chiplet, so a run that names that form under flow.packaging_flow.sheet.form draws the
# board from the same read. Each write of the .chiplet appends its sha before and after, and the
# form that made it, to runs/.written/<name>.jsonl beside it, keeping a copy of the form under
# runs/.written/forms/. A classic sheet read again is checked against that form when the ledger has
# it (otherwise the edit says it reads the sheet with no reference check), so a symbol KiCad
# re-annotated is refused instead of read as a new die. place passes interposer-pnr --rotations
# whenever the .chiplet has an external profile, with only the turn the terminal checks accept (0,
