STAGE_PACKAGING_FLOW_PATH=packaging-flow
# An earlier move was packaging-flow dev after the v2026.11 release, on top of the pin before it: the
# v2026.11 release bytes (its public references), then verify's text headlines
# pinned to its verdict words, with the not-all-built verdict said as a sentence.
#
# An earlier move (56affc12) added nine commits. Every spec's interposer passes the PDK plane as
# attachment_surface_z, so each design.chiplet it elaborates declares its mount plane; that field
# comes from chiplet-spec's system description, so this entry needs a
# STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_REF that carries it (pip check cannot see it). The system.terminal_map kind
# gains a bumps role and keys bumps, traces and route by projection, and the external signature
# keys the map by projection, so a rerun in another folder keeps its key; the registry digest moves
# (cc5f8010 to fe140b58), so terminal-map keys recorded before this ref no longer match. The
# example 8 terminal-map fixtures are taken again from a run whose writers declare the plane. No
# pyproject dependency moves; the version stays 0.1.0.
#
# This ref (53a2ce8) is packaging-flow's local dev, on the previous pin, and changes tests only.
# REAL_TREE_SHA256, the digest of the interposer DRC deck's file set, takes the tools/OpenIntM4TM2
# gitlink's d69aab4 tree (its 6_9_solderbump.drc changed; the set and its count did not), and the
# fixture PROVENANCE.md that states the live-roots pin names it, so this entry and that gitlink
# move together. One declared skip is dropped, test_pad_pitch's, and test_console changes. Nothing
# under src/ moves, no pyproject dependency moves, tests/fixtures/interposer_pdk and
# tests/die_flow/PROVENANCE.md are unchanged, and no test id in ci/required-tests.txt,
# ci/tracked-tree-tests.txt or ci/image-xor-tests.txt moves. The assembly-lvs, chiplet-flow and
# chiplet-spec entries that name packaging-flow do not move for it. The version stays 0.1.0.
STAGE_PACKAGING_FLOW_REF=53a2ce80835c8b3248101460d9af782926fe608f

# The die flow: one die per command, distribution chiplet-die-flow, console script
# `chiplet-die-flow` (chiplet_die_flow.die_cli:main). In the image the `chiplet-die-flow` on PATH is
# bin/chiplet-die-flow, which execs the venv's script. The tree, its staged path and the
# STAGE_CHIPLET_FLOW_* names keep the name chiplet-flow. build-die builds one die with LibreLane and
# writes its build.json, die view and ready die; die-view, emit-ready-die and eda-views each run one
# of those steps. run, resume, status and verify are packaging-flow's: here each prints one line on
# stderr naming packaging-flow and exits 1. build-die exits 0 when build.json is complete, 1 on a
# typed refusal and 7 on an internal error; the other commands add 6 and 11 for the two refusals
# with a code of their own. The same distribution installs librelane_plugin_chiplet, the plugin that
# registers the `Chiplet` LibreLane flow and its steps when LibreLane finds it on its import path.
#
# Installed editable for the same reason as packaging-flow: build-die records its own package's
# identity with `git rev-parse HEAD` on the directory find_spec resolves to. Package data is
# klayout_scripts/*.py. It runs no interposer-pnr engine and no assembly-lvs; those are
# packaging-flow's.
#
# Declared runtime deps are pyyaml>=6.0 and chiplet-format-io>=1.12.0 (the KLayout thread key and
# check options are read per PDK, PORTS_NOT_CHECKED names the deck trees that do not check the port
# labels, and eda-views read() returns only the reader's READ_KINDS, so the transistor-level kinds
# 1.12.0 adds are skipped and its keys do not change); the chiplet-full extra adds the reader's
# strict extra. When the recorded LVS deck tree is one of those and the deck log prints neither port
# line, emit-ready-die writes the ports run check as not_checked in a checks 1.1 block; every other
# build record keeps its checks 1.0 bytes. Loading the package imports chiplet_format_io (the
# builders read ready_die_names), so every chiplet-die-flow command and the LibreLane plugin load
# the reader. It no longer declares the system description, and its tests/test_module_boundary.py
# fails a file that imports it under either name. librelane and yamlcore are imported where used, in
# the die-build modules (LibreLane's environment). Its strict die-contract admission accepts strict
# capability revisions 3 and 4 until its own close to 4.
#
# The Nix build shell's LibreLane launcher puts this tree's src/ on LibreLane's import path
# (nix/build-env.sh's ADK_NIX_LAUNCHER_TREES, which names chiplet_die_flow and
# librelane_plugin_chiplet). The Nix CLI shell refuses for now: its tree table and the build-context
# capture (nix/capture_build_context.py, nix/capture_context.py) still name chiplet_flow and a
# chiplet-system tree, and nix/cli-env.sh stops on a row of its table whose ref this file no longer
# sets. It works again when those tables follow the rename.
#
# build-die runs LibreLane either as `cd $LIBRELANE_CHECKOUT && nix-shell --run "..."` or
# through an installed launcher named by CHIPLET_FLOW_LIBRELANE_LAUNCHER with its sha256 in
# CHIPLET_FLOW_LIBRELANE_LAUNCHER_SHA256 (both or neither), which is what the Nix build shell
# exports. So nix-shell or such a launcher has to exist where the build runs. This image carries
# no nix, but holds LibreLane, its interpreter, the sg13g2 PDK and such a launcher in its own
# /nix/store (nix/host-closure.lock) and sets the launcher pair in its ENV, so a die is built in it
# with no store of the host's; the image build itself builds none.
#
# emit-ready-die records the PDK revision from pdk-source.json at the PDK_ROOT the die's run
# resolved (format adk-tools-pdk-source/v1, written by nix/pdk-source.nix, schema in formats/)
# when that tree has one, else from git there; a statement that is present but invalid refuses.
# emit-eda-views does the same for a run on a store PDK, which has no checkout: it takes
# head_at_emit from the statement and records its digest as pdk.source_sha256, and refuses
# unless both match the build record. There a --supplementary file must equal the blob at the
# stated revision in a repository named with --pdk-git.
#
# emit-eda-views also records the die CDL, the LVS schematic the build's LVS step named, as cdl
# rows: every part of the build record's checks.lvs.reference, in its order, as
# cdl/<index>_<basename> carrying the part's role. It writes them only for a die whose LVS matched
# over the contract's layout with every run check at its passing value, and then all of them, each
# part at its reference digest (the parts outside the run also as chiplet.inputs-before.json
# declared them). Otherwise cdl goes under not_recorded with the first reason, the record is still
# written, and the command prints `cdl not recorded: <reason>`. read() skips the rows, since cdl
# is not among the reader's READ_KINDS, so what it returns does not change. The image's die views
# carry these rows (the "Die views" section).
#
# A die built with pad-side level shifters (its chiplet.inputs-before.json records pad_side) gets
# each shifter's views from the shifter's sealed drop, at the bytes the preparation receipt
# records: lef, liberty per corner and the netlist, which is written as a sim_model row ahead of
# the netlists. Every pad-side row is unqualified, a shifter takes no spef, and anything else in
# MACROS or a drop not sealed refuses. A die without shifters records what it did before.
#
# A die whose LVS compared a view written by klayout_scripts/flatten_fill_view.py (fill
# flattened, masters unified or both) rather than the layout itself gets an XOR of that view
# against the shipped GDS, run with the klayout on PATH (klayout_scripts/view_xor.py), when
# emit-ready-die writes its contract; the value lands in the die's LVS run checks. Verify step 4d
# runs tests/test_view_xor.py -m klayout under the image's KLayout, hands it the binary's real
# path and version (CHIPLET_FLOW_KLAYOUT_REALPATH, CHIPLET_FLOW_KLAYOUT_VERSION) and sets
# HCDP_STRICT_LIVE=1, under which chiplet-flow's conftest fails any skipped test; it also checks
# that `klayout -v` names the venv's klayout module version, which KLAYOUT_PIP pins. That module
# is the only part of chiplet-flow's suite the image runs. ci/image-xor-tests.txt lists its test
# ids (19 parametrised controls, X4, X5 and the emitter's binary test) and changes in the same
# commit as this pin whenever a control is added, renamed or removed.
#
# The repository's history was started again from one commit, whose tree carries the
# changes since the previous pin: GPL-2.0-or-later, with setuptools>=77 as the build backend,
# which the wheelhouse's 84.0.0 meets, the three vendored pads named, and build-die handing its
# LibreLane child the flow's plugin. The previous pin added five commits on it: host.py caps the
# thread count by the cgroup's cpu.max or the CPU affinity and records which one set it, and
# build-die takes its PDK root as --pdk-root, puts PDK_ROOT back after the build and refuses a
# split root. Both run wherever a die is built, the image included.
#
# It has been started again a second time: a root that holds the tree of the pin before
# it plus twenty-four commits. They rename the die flow to chiplet-die-flow (its
# distribution, console script and package; librelane_plugin_chiplet keeps its name), drop the
# system description from its dependencies, generate a bondpad-frame die's plan files from its
# configuration and refuse gf180 pads outside the die or overlapping, read the cgroup limits once so
# a dedicated machine in a capped container can leave the shared-host floor, reword the docs, and
# have the io_die example call die_view's public side_and_axis. tests/test_view_xor.py changes only
# in its two imports, so ci/image-xor-tests.txt does not change.
#
# An earlier move adds ten commits. They take internal references out of the docs,
# comments, tests and vehicle files (the DRIVER receipt goldens that hash those files are
# rewritten), drop the local verilog_closure copy and sixteen error classes nothing raises, stop a
# die command's tools with it on SIGTERM or SIGHUP and send a Ctrl-C on to the tools' process group
# before build-die waits, and name a package's commit only when its checkout tracks the package.
#
# An earlier move adds two commits. They relicense the die flow to GPL-3.0-or-later (LICENSE,
# SPDX lines, pyproject; the DRIVER goldens and byte pins that hash those headers are rewritten)
# and name in REUSE.toml the licence of the sealed level-shifter drops and the pad view, which
# carry no header.
#
# An earlier move adds two commits: the echo core's header comment (vehicle/rtl/echo_core.v)
# without planning labels, and the CX core's origin blob, which that comment moves.
#
# An earlier move adds eight commits. They add prepare-die, which prepares a die
# project from RTL in one command (a contract-bound core, or a stock core it reports unbound),
# writes its record into the die project, and the read-only check-die-project, which says whether
# a die project is still as prepared (exit 0 prepared, 1 changed, 2 not judged); build-die and
# prepare-die print their step progress on stderr and name librelane.log. A bondpad-frame plan's
# pin rows carry the port's direction and role, so vehicle/plans/driver_ls.pins.json and
# echo_gf180_cx.pins.json change bytes.
#
# An earlier move adds four documentation commits: a short README, the command
# and development pages under docs/, and a rewritten bondpad-frame template README; the old
# template materialization page is removed, and the walkthrough test stops reading it. No
# code changes.
#
# An earlier move adds five commits. emit-ready-die and build-die take
# --supply-volts, a file of supply volts by domain, and then stamp the die contract 1.2 with its
# supply_domains and record the file by name and sha256; without it the contract stays 1.1 and the
# bytes do not change. The strict die-contract reader admits capability revisions 3 and 4.
#
# An earlier move adds one CI commit: the host-tier workflow and its .github/ci/
# reader, report and judge, generated by the CI runner from the tier row; tests.yml drops the
# job and gate it replaces. No package or test file changes.
#
# An earlier move adds two commits. The preparation evidence counts prepare-die's record as the
# preparation's own file, so a prepared project no longer reports it as an operator addition; tests
# on the entry and flow paths pin that. The die-contract reader still admits revisions 3 and 4; the
# close to {4} (which also drops 3 from the bundle's supported cases) is a chiplet-flow commit a
# later pin takes.
#
# An earlier move adds one CI commit. tests.yml reads its chiplet-spec and
# IHP-Open-PDK commits through the generated .github/ci/read_pins.py (--names CHIPLET_SPEC_REF
# SG13G2_REF), itself regenerated by the CI runner, which can also name the image's IHP-Open-PDK
# and gf180mcu commits (SG13G2_REF, GF180MCU_REF); the repository's own .github/read_pins.py is
# gone, and tests/test_read_pins.py now holds the workflow to the generated reader. No package file
# changes.
#
# An earlier move adds 28 commits. chiplet-die-flow derive-padring-die writes a ready
# die from a run of LibreLane's stock Chip flow (the top, the bond pad masters with their LEFs and a
# source netlist), re-admitted by the strict reader; examples/io_die/derive_io.py is now its caller
# and writes the same bytes over the CF_IO run. The technology follows the run's PDK: DIE_VIEW_LAYERS
# gains an ihp-sg13cmos5l row (TopMetal1, Passiv) and a per-technology IO_SUPPLY_PINS table names
# the supply pads. ihp-sg13cmos5l also gets its bondpad master, ip/bondpad_70x70_5L_cf (an
# Apache-2.0 byte copy, with a TopMetal1 access LEF), its BONDPADS row and its LVS deck row. The
# dependency floor moves to chiplet-format-io 1.19.0, whose ihp-sg13cmos5l thread key the bondpad
# row reads at import, so this pin moves with STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_REF. tests/test_view_xor.py does
# not change, so ci/image-xor-tests.txt does not. packaging-flow's die-flow tests read this ref
# (its refusal classes and its test sources), so STAGE_PACKAGING_FLOW_REF moves with it.
#
# An earlier move adds 16 commits. An OpenROAD abstract step writes a core's LEF view
# (src/chiplet_die_flow/abstract_lef.py), and prepare-die stages it as the core LEF, with a
# vehicle's own routing-track file, and puts the plugin on a stock core run's path. The vehicle is
# the picorv32a bondpad die on ihp-sg13cmos5l: its vendored RTL, a Magic-free stock-flow core
# project kept self-contained (a stock core that reads a file outside its directory is refused),
# one prepare-die command that builds it, and tests that run no EDA. A DRC count on the bare core
# no longer stops its run; the final die GDS, core included, is where DRC decides. No package
# dependency moves, and tests/test_view_xor.py does not change, so
# ci/image-xor-tests.txt does not. packaging-flow's die-flow tests read this ref, so
# STAGE_PACKAGING_FLOW_REF moves with it.
#
# An earlier move adds ten commits. chiplet-die-flow pad-frame writes a ready die that
# holds bond pads and nothing else, from a frame spec (chiplet_die_flow.pad_frame version 1); its
# build record says pad-frame-not-a-circuit, qualified false. The pad master, its layers and the PDK
# come from technology rows, so the command names no technology. pad-frame and derive-padring-die
# now write the die directory through one writer (src/chiplet_die_flow/ready_die_dir.py: a hidden
# folder renamed into place once the strict reader admits it, removed on any failure);
# emit_ready_die keeps its own path. die_view.number_pads, which both share, lets a pad's alias be
# its own name. A malformed frame spec is refused as PadFrameRefused. No package dependency moves,
# the reader floor stays chiplet-format-io 1.20.0, and tests/test_view_xor.py does not change, so
# ci/image-xor-tests.txt does not. packaging-flow names every die-flow refusal class, so
# STAGE_PACKAGING_FLOW_REF moves with it.
#
# This ref (54f256d) adds one test-only commit on the previous pin: tests/test_die_view.py renames a test
# pad whose name read like a phase tag. No package file moves, tests/test_view_xor.py does not
# change, so ci/image-xor-tests.txt does not. packaging-flow's die-flow provenance names it, so
# STAGE_PACKAGING_FLOW_REF moves with it.
STAGE_CHIPLET_FLOW_PATH=chiplet-flow
# This ref (54f256d) is chiplet-flow dev after the v2026.11 release, on the previous pin
# 54f256d: one commit, the v2026.11 release bytes (its public references); no code moves.
STAGE_CHIPLET_FLOW_REF=54f256d55635d7aadd217a237ca591dfb1601108

# Co-simulation over the dies a flow emitted: distribution chiplet-cosim, console script
# `chiplet-cosim` (chiplet_cosim.cli:main: system-top, xschem-sheet, xschem-read, from-run, select,
# readings, setup-project, setup-import, setup-xschem-sheets and setup-xschem-read; the image has no
# Xschem). The venv carries the script; nothing in bin/ wraps it. Declared runtime deps are
# pyyaml>=6.0, chiplet-system-description and chiplet-format-io[strict]>=1.15.0; the strict extra's
# jsonschema is already in the venv. Loading the CLI imports chiplet_system_description and PyYAML
# and nothing else third-party; cocotb is imported only by the driver, observer, library and
# mixed-run modules. Package data is cosim_engine_capability.json, cosim_readings.json (the readings
# table, which chiplet_cosim.readings reads on import), cosim_setup_symbols.json,
# diagnostics/*.json, hv_gate_classes.json and hv_gate_roles.json. The cocotb modules run under the
# co-simulation engine's own python3.13 (the "Co-simulation engine" section below), never under the
# venv: the venv's interpreter runs the CLI and starts that one.
#
# from-run runs the case a finished run's dies select on the gate views --view-store holds for them
# (an absolute path to an existing folder; the image's is $ADK_DIE_VIEWS) and writes nothing inside
# the run or the store; its exit table adds 3 (the record reads FAIL) and 6 (inconclusive) to 0, 1,
# 2, 4, 5 and 130, and on 0, 1, 3, 4, 5 and 6 its last act writes <out>/from-run.json beside a copy
# of the readings table. Its cases are CF_DRIVER_BONDPAD with CF_ECHO_BONDPAD, both sg13g2;
# CF_DRIVER_BONDPAD with CF_ECHO_CX_BONDPAD, a gf180mcu ECHO; and CF_DRIVER_LS, the DRIVER rebuilt
# with level shifters at its pads, with that same CF_ECHO_CX_BONDPAD. The image's die views (the
# "Die views" section) hold six records, CF_DRIVER_BONDPAD, CF_ECHO_BONDPAD, CF_DRIVER_LS,
# CF_ECHO_CX_BONDPAD and the sigma-delta pair SD_CORE_BONDPAD and SD_AFE_BONDPAD, each at the one
# layout its record binds. A die at any other layout has no views there: two_die_echo_gf180's
# CF_ECHO_BONDPAD, built on gf180mcu, and the gf180mcu chip_top mixed_gf180 places, among others.
# The gf180mcu cell models come from CF_ECHO_CX_BONDPAD's own record (sim_model/), not from a PDK
# root. The capability table is at version 0.3, each entry of kind gate or mixed. Its second gate
# entry, sg13g2 with the gf180mcu 7t5v0 cells, names the same Icarus, VPI module, cocotb and
# observer as sg13g2.stdcell.icarus13. The verify stage checks that engine against
# sg13g2.stdcell.icarus13, which table 0.3 changed only by naming its kind, and runs none of this
# package's tests. Each mixed entry admits runs only in the exact context it records (the hashes of
# its tools, VPI modules, interpreter and models), and the verify stage does not check them. There
# are three: two_tech_loop and the sd_built case at two corners, sd_built_tt and sd_built_ss, each
# prefixed with the verify image it was recorded in; a context byte that moves makes their runs
# refuse. The mixed case (mixed_run) reads its tools from COSIM_NGSPICE_PREFIX, COSIM_IVERILOG,
# COSIM_LIBVVP and COSIM_COCOTB_PYTHON, which the image does not set; the mixed-signal engine's
# files are linked under /opt/adk-tools/mixsim-devshell. It reads a gf180mcuD models entry's root
# from COSIM_GF180_ROOT alone, which the image sets to /opt/adk-tools/gf180mcu/gf180mcuD:
# design.ngspice and sm141064.ngspice at the GF180 sheet's commit, and no cell library. An
# ihp-sg13g2 entry's root is $PDK_ROOT/ihp-sg13g2; into that slice the image adds, at SG13G2_REF,
# the files sg13g2-models.sha256 lists (every file the MOS, diode, resistor and capacitor corner
# libraries include, in any section and at any depth, since ngspice reads a library whole, and the
# stdcell spice), and copies of four of the engine's OSDI files under libs.tech/ngspice/osdi
# (psp103, psp103_nqs, r3_cmc, mosvar), since the model walk refuses an OSDI file that resolves
# outside the PDK installation. The case reads the Icarus shim's time reversal line, in either
# window, as an engine error (INCONCLUSIVE), and its diagnostics/ngspice.json names tolerated pairs
# besides tolerated lines: ngspice's dynamic gmin start and completion notes are tolerated only as a
# pair. The vehicle's committed lock records the harness locations of the host shell it was planned
# in, so a plan of it in the image reads them as moved; the verify stage does not read the lock.
#
# select says which case a finished run's dies select and gives that case's key, as from-run would
# read the run and the store, without copying, locking or running anything; from-run compares a
# version 4 manifest's recorded select answer with its own before it copies. readings prints the
# readings table.
#
# The history starts at a new root. It reads the description under its new name
# (chiplet_system_description, also in the mixed case's PACKAGES, the packages a mixed verdict's
# identity hashes), guards the die flow under both names, and sets the reader floor at 1.14.1. The
# mixed case re-admits by case key, takes .meas tran cards after the bench's subcircuit and records
# their values (a card that reads anything but v() is refused, and a run that never read ngspice's
# output writes its measures as null), and writes waves.raw and waves.vcd when the case asks for
# waves; a cocotb run can dump its waves as VCD. A version 0.6 config may probe nodes inside a
# block, and from-run takes a die whose blocks read view files beside the config, which needs its
# store directory only. from-run and select take packaging-flow's version 5 manifest as version 4
# (its halt:<level> rows and gates object are not read) and hold it to its recorded select answer.
# xschem-read writes the connectivity form and stops, and refuses a --base that is not the sheet's.
# The package has the co-sim setup: a canonical element form of a bench, a writer for mixed configs,
# setup-project and setup-import, and Xschem setup sheets with their read-back (setup-xschem-sheets,
# setup-xschem-read), each refusing a setup revision the lock would refuse. The setup modules import
# chiplet_system_description.connectivity and chiplet_format_io.transistor_view, which the
# chiplet-spec entry's tree has (description/ and reference/python). The package is
# GPL-3.0-or-later, two modules and the copied formats staying Apache-2.0, and its build needs
# setuptools 77 or later, which the 84.0.0 wheelhouse is. The runner's digest has moved since the
# capability table's mixed entries were recorded, so they admit no run until entries recorded
# against this ref replace them; that check does not compare the runner. The code that check calls
# (mixed_run.Tools, the spinit template, mixed_case.roots_from), and the observer and gate entries
# verify step 8 reads, are unchanged since those entries were recorded. The verify stage runs none
# of select, readings or from-run.
#
# xschem-sheet given a die's --views (the eda-views.json copy from-run wrote) and its --contract
# also draws that die below its symbol from the record's cdl rows, and proves the drawing equal to
# the rows with Xschem, the one NIX_XSCHEM_OUTPATH names in the image; without --views it draws
# only the system sheet. The verify stage runs none of it.
#
# The schematic commands import chiplet_system_description.connectivity, from the chiplet-spec
# entry's description/. It is installed editable like every staged tree. The Nix CLI
# shell does not install it: nothing that shell runs imports it.
#
# An earlier move adds 14 commits. They relicense the repository to
# GPL-3.0-or-later and refresh the co-simulation identities the header change moved, re-lock
# after chiplet-flow's reworded vehicle and echo core comments, read the flow's manifest at the
# commit its sibling tree holds, and move the reference from the README to docs/.
#
# An earlier move adds two commits: the strict die-contract reader reads exactly
# capability revision 4, the one the chiplet-spec entry carries, and its test of that set says
# which of its two conditions failed; the reader floor moves to 1.15.0.
#
# An earlier move adds one CI commit: the host-tier workflow and its .github/ci/ reader, report
# and judge, generated by the CI runner from the tier row; tests.yml drops the job and gate it
# replaces. No package or test file changes.
#
# An earlier move adds one CI commit. tests.yml reads its chiplet-spec, IHP-Open-PDK
# and gf180mcu commits through the generated .github/ci/read_pins.py (--names CHIPLET_SPEC_REF
# SG13G2_REF GF180MCU_REF), itself regenerated by the CI runner, which can also name the image's
# IHP-Open-PDK and gf180mcu commits (SG13G2_REF, GF180MCU_REF); the repository's own
# .github/read_pins.py is gone, and tests/test_read_pins.py now holds the workflow to the generated
# reader. No package file changes.
#
# An earlier move adds six test commits. tests/test_cosim_run_seam.py waits on each
# seam event instead of a fixed window, with split deadlines, and kills what a failed wait leaves.
# The mixed timeout case in tests/test_mixed_run.py times only the simulator, fails when the
# process group outlives the kill, and runs to tran_stop 1e-2 instead of 1e-3, so an unkilled run
# lands an order of magnitude past the bound, its 2 s timeout plus KILL_SLACK_S (60 s). No package
# file changes.
#
# This ref (dcd7fdc) adds four test commits: the gate and capability tests check each mixed
# entry's id by its form (a verify image, then its case) and hold it to the image its property
# names, instead of spelling the image tag, and the two_tech_loop context fixture drops the tag
# from its file name. No package file changes.
STAGE_CHIPLET_COSIM_PATH=chiplet-cosim
# An earlier move (dcd7fdc) was chiplet-cosim dev after the v2026.11 release, on the pin before
# it, dcd7fdc: one commit, the v2026.11 release bytes (its public references).
#
# This ref (50247f2) adds two data commits: vehicle/io_ring_two_die.chiplet declares the
# interposer's attachment_surface_z (so the lock's assembly digest and the test's DOCUMENT_SHA
# move), and the Xschem sheet fixture is taken again from a design that declares its plane (its
# netlists unchanged). No package file, pyproject or version moves.
STAGE_CHIPLET_COSIM_REF=50247f2c4e3441dd28c5de3fb3c4f82c650051eb

# chiplet-spec: the .chiplet reference reader and writer, installed as the distribution
# chiplet-format-io from reference/python with the `strict` extra, and the system description,
# installed as chiplet-system-description from description/, both editable off this staged tree. Its
# remote is renamed chiplet-system-spec with the push of this ref, the name ci/integration-refs.json
# and the integration workflows give it, so adk-tools is pushed after it; the folder, the pin key
# and the STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_* names keep chiplet-spec. The whole repository is staged, not only
# reference/python, because in a checkout the reader loads the canonical schemas/ from the
# repository root. No console scripts. It is the top-level `chiplet_format_io` in the venv: the
# ADK's strict admission imports it, packaging-flow and chiplet-flow import it when they load (the
# shared names in ready_die_names) and in their strict readers, chiplet-cosim imports it where used,
# and interposer-pnr's version checks use its check_contract_version because it is importable here.
#
# The system description (description/, Apache-2.0) is the typed Python eDSL that elaborates a
# chiplet-on-interposer system spec to .chiplet: distribution chiplet-system-description, package
# chiplet_system_description, and one console script, `chiplet-system-description`, which
# bin/chiplet-system-description execs and verify step 14 runs on a source system.chiplet the
# description's own test helper writes (`check` reads a source and its die folders, tiers 0 and 1,
# and starts no EDA tool; its report keeps the format name chiplet_system.check). Its one runtime
# dependency, PyYAML, is already in the image venv. packaging-flow and chiplet-cosim declare it
# and import it when their CLIs load; the die flow neither declares nor imports it. It moved here
# from its own repository, so this entry is now its one pin. packaging-flow
# lifts board and sheet edits with chiplet_system_description.source.edit and emits .chiplet with
# chiplet_system_description.emit, which pip check cannot see, so the packaging-flow pin and this
# one move together. It carries its own copy of the .chiplet reader under a private name,
# src/chiplet_system_description/_vendor/chiplet_format_io (see the copies below), through which
# emit writes. A Technology may name a layer sheet and a stackup YAML, which elaborate writes as
# given, only when set, and refuses as relative paths. Its examples mount the dies on the
# interposer PDK's beol.attachment_surface_z_um, read from
# $INTERPOSER_PDK_ROOT/libs.tech/intm4tm2_stack.json; its tests read a byte copy,
# description/tests/fixtures/interposer_pdk/libs.tech/intm4tm2_stack.json, with a PROVENANCE.md,
# and the verify stage's step 0c holds that copy to the PDK this image carries, so a PDK pin that
# moves the file fails the build until the description refreshes its copy. The verify stage runs
# none of its tests.
#
# Reader history, oldest first. 1.12.0 carries the strict die-contract admission of 1.8.0 unchanged
# (STRICT_ADMISSION_API_VERSION 3, SUPPORTED_DIE_CONTRACT_VERSION "1.1", accepted contract versions
# ("1.1",)), the modules the split flows share names through since 1.9.0 (ready_die_names,
# eda_views, pins, technology, verilog_closure), and in ready_die_names the gf180mcuD check rows,
# with DRC_THREADS and DRC_OPTIONS keyed by PDK name and a new DRC_REQUIRED (1.10.0), and checks 1.1
# with PORTS_NOT_CHECKED (1.11.0). Those two 1.10.0 names changed shape, so a flat reader of them
# breaks; in the image only packaging-flow and chiplet-flow read them, both at refs written for it.
# 1.12.0 adds the transistor-level view of a block: eda_views.KINDS gains cdl, spice and
# transistor_view, read() builds from the new READ_KINDS (the 1.11.0 kinds) so its result keys do
# not change, a transistor_view module reads a chiplet-eda-views-transistor/v1 view, and
# TECHNOLOGY_OF_PDK maps ihp-sg13cmos5l. The rev also carries the coordinate frame contract's
# IntM4TM2 attach plane, which ci/check_attach_plane.py holds the image's .chiplet files to.
#
# Reader 1.13.0 adds three things and changes no name an earlier release exports. A new agreement
# module reads a spice transistor view against the CDL an LVS run took as its reference.
# ready_die_names gains run_check_not_counted, the one copy of the rule for which value of an LVS
# run check counts. transistor_view gains derive, which returns the pins, local subckts and devices
# that the reader checks a view's meta against, so a writer can state them; and cdl rows now split
# into views all or none, so a record with no cdl view reads its spice view. Strict admission stays
# at revision 3. chiplet-cosim needs 1.13.0 for that spice read. At the refs staged here
# chiplet-cosim and packaging-flow declare chiplet-format-io>=1.15.0, the first with revision 4,
# and the die flow >=1.12.0, so pip check reads those floors and a ref below 1.15.0 fails the
# image's install step. Four consumers in the image select a strict API revision:
# checks/assembly_admission.py at the tools/adk gitlink, and SUPPORTED_REVISIONS in the
# strict_reader.py of packaging-flow, chiplet-flow and chiplet-cosim. A reader at another revision
# needs all four to move with it.
#
# Reader 1.14.0 adds format 1.1: a source document, one with a top-level system block, is stamped
# 1.1 (SOURCE_FORMAT_VERSION) and an assembly stays stamped 1.0 (SUPPORTED_FORMAT_VERSION);
# ACCEPTED_FORMAT_VERSIONS moves to ("1.1",), so an assembly stamped 1.1 reads with no warning. A
# reader that wants an assembly refuses a source document with SourceDocumentError, a subclass of
# ChipletFormatError, whatever allow_intermediate says. assembly.units is closed to um (absent means
# um) and refused otherwise, and solder_bump is a 1.1 member of interfaces[].type, refused in a
# document stamped below 1.1 (MEMBER_SINCE). Strict admission stays at revision 3 and the die
# contract at 1.1.
#
# An earlier move carries reader 1.14.1 and the system description, which reads a blank system
# sheet and compares two sources by their nets (source.compare). Its ancestor reader
# 1.13.1 is a squash onto the remote's dev 3340e11. 1.14.1 rewords the comments in both readers and
# their tests and names the source reader by its new package in the source refusal; no verdict,
# writer output or API moves.
#
# An earlier move adds 63 commits. Reader 1.15.0 adds format 1.2, a declared volts on
# a power rail, moves the die contract to 1.2 with the die's supply_domains, and strict admission to
# revision 4; 1.0 and 1.1 contracts keep every verdict, and one stamped 1.2 or above may move
# (revision 3 admits a 1.3 contract whose ground domain is at 1.5 V, 4 refuses it). Reader 1.16.0
# adds format 1.3, the unplaced source, which the description reads, places and writes back;
# ACCEPTED_FORMAT_VERSIONS moves to ("1.3",), an assembly carrying an unplaced block is refused, and
# an assembly is still stamped 1.0. It also refuses some 1.1 and 1.2 sources the earlier reader took:
# a die with no at, a carrier with no size, an external with no edges, or an unplaced
# key; packaging-flow reads sources through the description, so it meets them there. The description
# reads sources through its vendored 1.16.0 reader, checks a rail's volts against its dies'
# supply_domains, and numbers sheet pins from a die contract's terminals (pad_numbers). The four
# strict consumers move with it: the ADK admits exactly revision 4, packaging-flow and chiplet-cosim
# read exactly {4}, and chiplet-flow reads {3, 4} until its own close. The last two commits, after
# release 1.16.0, state the licence as plain Apache-2.0 and reword a schema description;
# no reader byte moves.
#
# An earlier move adds four commits. Reader 1.17.0 adds footprint_ids, the footprint
# names a KiCad sheet gives a die (chiplet_dies:<cell>) and an external pad
# (chiplet_carrier:EXT_PAD_<um>um), refusing a cell KiCad 9 would not keep, an empty name and any
# whitespace; gds_to_kicad's die-folder handout (die_handout.py) names its footprints through it.
# No format, contract or strict revision moves in the reader, and __init__.py changes only its
# release string. The description does move a verdict: every path of it that reads a die folder
# (read_die_folder: the classic import, source plan, so check, validate, place and compare, and
# convert) now refuses a die contract with no version or another major, and warns on a newer
# minor, where it read the contract before. packaging-flow meets it through compare-nets. The
# description does not vendor footprint_ids.
#
# An earlier move adds two commits, both under conformance/: the manifest holds
# interposer-pnr's reader to the unplaced refusal and says how ir's base error is named. No
# reader byte moves.
#
# An earlier move adds four commits, all documents: the README becomes a short newcomer guide,
# the coordinate-frame contract keeps only its normative part and names the reference tools rather
# than their source files, and the format spec states format 1.3 at its head. No reader, schema or
# conformance byte moves.
#
# An earlier move adds two commits. The description's reader of the connectivity form
# (chiplet_system.connectivity) reads its minor 1.3, at which gds_to_kicad writes classic sheets;
# 1.3 adds each classic symbol's units and jumpers, neither of which the description's classic
# import reads, so a classic form reads as a 1.2 one and no longer warns on every import. The form
# version the connectivity module writes (its VERSION) moves from 1.2 to 1.3, and chiplet-cosim's
# xschem-read stamps it into the forms it writes, so those now say 1.3; nothing pins that value.
# docs/VERSION_POLICY.md says which minor each sheet kind is written at. No chiplet_format_io
# reader, schema or conformance byte moves.
#
# An earlier move adds six commits. Format 1.4 is implemented in both reference
# readers (release 1.18.0): a die row of a source may name its admission file,
# system.dies[].admission (a relative .json, no '..' segment, holding reason, scope and
# not_verified), for a die the run does not build. A source stamped below 1.4 that writes the key is
# refused naming the minor; a reader before 1.4 skips the item key, so the die is not admitted. The
# schema carries the key with x-since, and a conformance test holds schema and reader to one verdict
# per stamp. The description reads a die's admission in the source and keeps it when a classic sheet
# is read again; its vendored __init__.py moves with the reader. STRICT_ADMISSION_API_VERSION stays
# 4. Every Python copy of the reader held byte-equal to this tree moves to 1.18.0 with this ref
# (below), so interposer-pnr's entry and the tools/gds_to_kicad and tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin
# gitlinks move with it, and packaging-flow's, whose source loader reads the key.
#
# An earlier move adds six commits and is reader release 1.19.0. ready_die_names
# gains the ihp-sg13cmos5l rows of the KLayout check tables. They are read off the ihp-sg13cmos5l
# decks at IHP-Open-PDK 22f43352 and kept apart from the ihp-sg13g2 rows: unlike the ihp-sg13g2
# one, that main DRC deck has no default for run_mode, so its row requires the key. The release
# string is the one change to __init__.py, in the description's vendored copy too, and the C++
# reader's version moves with it. No schema byte moves; the conformance writer pin names the
# release. STRICT_ADMISSION_API_VERSION stays 4. Every Python copy of the reader held byte-equal
# to this tree moves to 1.19.0 with this ref (below), so interposer-pnr's entry and the
# tools/gds_to_kicad and tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin gitlinks move with it, and packaging-flow's and
# chiplet-flow's, which read the new rows.
#
# An earlier move adds two commits and is reader release 1.20.0, format 1.5. A rail
# of the system block may state its routing requirement, system.rails[].width_um, or amps with
# current_density_ma_per_um, on a power or ground rail. The source path refuses a broken pairing,
# or a value that is not a positive finite number, at every minor, and a source stamped below 1.5
# that writes one of the keys is refused naming the minor. The schema carries the keys with
# x-since; both readers accept 1.5, so 1.6 is the first minor that warns, and the C++ reader moves
# its accepted minor and release only. The description reads a rail's requirement and its
# elaborator hands it over as Extras.rail_requirements, never in the System. __init__.py is the one
# reader file that changes, in the description's vendored copy too. STRICT_ADMISSION_API_VERSION
# stays 4. Every Python copy of the reader held byte-equal to this tree moves to 1.20.0 with this
# ref (below), so interposer-pnr's entry and the tools/gds_to_kicad and tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin
# gitlinks move with it, and packaging-flow's, whose dependency floors name 1.20.0.
#
# An earlier move adds one commit and is reader release 1.21.0, format 1.5. A new
# sheet_names module holds the rule a KiCad sheet's net, pin and pad names are written and read
# back with: a plain first character, then plain characters and [N] digit subscripts, so a die's
# bus pad such as U1_IO_BOND_bidirs[0].bidir_pad reads back instead of being refused as
# unmappable_name; a range, an empty, nested or non-digit subscript, or a bracket first is refused.
# The description's schematic and classic imports read a sheet's names with it, and gds_to_kicad's
# sheet generator and die handout write with it; every other check keeps connectivity.NAME_RULE,
# which does not change. No format version, schema byte or STRICT_ADMISSION_API_VERSION (4) moves;
# __init__.py changes its release string only, and the C++ reader its READER_RELEASE and CMake
# VERSION, which nothing in the image builds. Every Python copy of the reader held byte-equal to
# this tree moves to 1.21.0 with this ref (below), so interposer-pnr's entry and the
# tools/gds_to_kicad and tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin gitlinks move with it; the description's and
# gds_to_kicad's copies gain sheet_names.py, which step 0b's subset rule admits because this tree's
# reference carries it. packaging-flow's floors stay at 1.20.0, which this release meets, as do
# chiplet-flow's (1.19.0) and chiplet-cosim's (1.15.0), so those entries need not move for it.
#
# An earlier move adds two commits, both in the description: elaboration refuses a source whose
# carrier is not IntM4TM2, in plain words. reference/python does not change, so the reader stays
# release 1.21.0 and every copy held byte-equal to it stays current. interposer-pnr's and
# gds_to_kicad's SOURCE files keep naming the earlier commit they were copied from; that move keeps its reader files; the plugin
# re-vendored there, so the tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin gitlink moved with it. packaging-flow
# refuses the same carriers at spec load, so its entry moved with this one.
#
# An earlier move (6f8e0dc) adds one commit on the previous pin, on the release line rather than
# dev, whose 1.22.0 that release leaves out: its schemas/chiplet_pads.json takes the ADK's 0.3.0 bytes,
# so the chiplet_pads vocabulary that ci/contract-registry.json holds byte-identical to the
# tools/adk gitlink's config/chiplet_pads.json matches again. Only the version line moves.
# reference/python does not change, so the reader stays release 1.21.0 and every copy held
# byte-equal to it stays current. The plugin's vendor/chiplet_format_io.SOURCE names the commit of
# its copy, and its pin tests require the staged ref to be that commit, so the
# tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin gitlink moves with it. The ADK's vendor/README.md declares the commit
# of its copy the same way, and its guard fails when the staged checkout lacks that commit (the
# image names this tree as CHIPLET_SPEC_ROOT), so the tools/adk gitlink moves with it as well, or
# the commit rides along as an extra.
#
# An earlier move (7031df5) was the merge of the v2026.11 release into dev and reader release 1.22.0.
# __init__.py changes its release string only; the new technology_package.py validator and its
# schema are in this tree and no copy takes them (step 0b lets a copy omit a reference file). No
# format version, existing schema byte or STRICT_ADMISSION_API_VERSION (4) moves. Every Python copy
# held byte-equal to this tree moves to 1.22.0 with this ref, and each SOURCE or README now names
# 7031df5: interposer-pnr's entry and the tools/adk, tools/gds_to_kicad and
# tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin gitlinks move with it. packaging-flow's floors (1.20.0),
# chiplet-flow's (1.19.0) and chiplet-cosim's (1.15.0) are met, so those entries do not move.
#
# An earlier move (48a3052) carried the mount-plane work on top of the 1.22.0 reader: the file's
# attachment_surface_z is the only mount plane (an exporter refuses an interposer that seats dies
# without it; neither dimensions.thickness nor the stackup stands in for it), and a die's z adds
# the interposer's position.z. The description's Interposer takes an optional
# attachment_surface_z, which packaging-flow's specs pass, so its entry moves with this
# one; the description's fixtures and KiCad forms declare it, and conformance/fixtures/mount_plane
# holds the cases each tool that computes z runs. reference/python/chiplet_format_io and schemas/
# do not change, so the reader stays 1.22.0, STRICT_ADMISSION_API_VERSION 4, and every copy below
# stays byte-equal. The plugin re-declares its copy at this ref, and the ADK's vendor/README.md
# names it. interposer-pnr's and gds_to_kicad's SOURCE files keep naming 7031df5, whose reader
# files are this ref's byte for byte; 7031df5 is not in this ref's history and not on
# chiplet-spec's remote. They are aligned at the next reader move.
#
# The ADK's strict admission refuses its own vendored copy on purpose: when chiplet_format_io
# resolves into the ADK's vendor/, load_admission() refuses and admission reports UNDECIDABLE,
# because that copy has no die_contract.py and no shipped schema. Without this entry the strict
# tests in the ADK's assembly-admission suite skip. The verify stage calls load_admission() before
# it runs that suite, so a missing or incompatible reader fails the build instead of skipping.
#
# `strict` adds jsonschema>=4, already satisfied by the venv's jsonschema 4.26.0, so it brings no
# new distribution. It is named anyway: the reader refuses strict admission when jsonschema is
# absent rather than degrading, and the extra is what declares that dependency. The Nix CLI shell
# (nix/cli-env.sh) installs the same reference/python editable with --no-deps and no extra, takes
# jsonschema from Nix, and exports CHIPLET_SPEC_ROOT as the tree; in its evidence mode that tree
# is staging/chiplet-spec and must be at this ref. The Nix build shell's LibreLane launcher also
# puts this tree's reference/python on LibreLane's import path (nix/build-env.sh).
#
# This ref (9da50b2) adds the v2026.11.1 release merges, a format 1.2 reader test run from a
# vendored copy, and reworded comments and docs. reference/python/chiplet_format_io, schemas/ and
# conformance/fixtures/mount_plane are byte-identical to 48a3052's, so the reader stays 1.22.0
# and every copy below stays byte-equal; description/src changes two comments only. The image and
# the integration job stage this tree one commit deep, so 48a3052 is not there: the plugin
# re-declares its copy at this ref and the ADK's vendor/README.md and MOUNT_PLANE_PIN name it, so
# the tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin and tools/adk gitlinks move with it.
#
# The image holds six Python copies of chiplet_format_io, three of them strict (strict: the copy
# carries die_contract.py with STRICT_ADMISSION_API_VERSION): the installed one, whose revision the
# strict consumers select, and the description's and gds_to_kicad's, which read die contracts
# through their own copies and select no revision. Measured at the refs this file and the
# adk-tools gitlinks name; re-measure when any of them moves:
#
#   - installed from this entry: 1.22.0, strict, revision 4; what the strict consumers import.
#   - the system description, description/src/chiplet_system_description/_vendor (a private
#     name, never on sys.path): 1.22.0, from this ref, since it sits in this tree: __init__.py,
#     die_contract.py, pad_numbers.py, sheet_names.py, supply_domains.py and technology.py,
#     byte-identical to this tree's reference/python ones (step 0b checks it).
#     Its emit, which packaging-flow calls, writes .chiplet documents through it.
#   - packaging-flow, chiplet-flow and chiplet-cosim carry no copy; they import the installed one.
#   - interposer-pnr, src/interposer_pnr/_vendor/chiplet_format_io (a private name): 1.22.0,
#     __init__.py and footprint_ids.py, byte-identical to this ref's and to 7031df5's, the commit
#     its _vendor/chiplet_format_io.SOURCE declares. Its form export names footprints through it.
#     interposer-pnr's own test_sheet_form holds the copy to its SOURCE, and step 0b holds it to
#     this tree.
#   - tools/adk, vendor/: 1.22.0, __init__.py only, byte-identical to this ref's (its
#     vendor/README.md declares this ref, and the ADK's tests/test_chiplet2dbx.py compares the
#     copy with this ref's blob in the staged tree, and its tests/fixtures/mount_plane with this
#     ref's cases). The contract registry compares it with this
#     entry's reader byte for byte (a mirror of cfio_python_reader).
#   - tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin, plugins/chiplet_export/vendor: 1.22.0, __init__.py only,
#     byte-identical to this ref's (its vendor/chiplet_format_io.SOURCE names this ref, and its
#     tests hold the copy and their oracle fixture to it; its Apache-2.0 text is
#     LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt at the plugin root). Step 0b does not pass this copy to
#     check_vendored_reader; the export path (hyp_to_gds.py) loads .chiplet documents and stamps
#     format_version through it.
#     The contract registry compares it with this entry's reader byte for byte (a mirror of
#     cfio_python_reader).
#   - tools/gds_to_kicad, _vendor/chiplet_format_io (a private name): 1.22.0, __init__.py,
#     die_contract.py, footprint_ids.py, pad_numbers.py, sheet_names.py, supply_domains.py and
#     technology.py, byte-identical to this tree's reference/python ones (step 0b checks it),
#     copied from 7031df5, the commit its _vendor/chiplet_format_io.SOURCE declares. Its
#     die-folder handout (die_handout.py) reads die contracts and names footprints through it.
#
# The tree also carries description/tests/fixtures/reader_1_2, the description's format 1.2 reader
# kept for its 1.2 test, with its own _vendor/chiplet_format_io. It is deliberately not this ref's
# reader and nothing imports it outside that test, which holds it to its own SHA256SUMS; it is not
# counted among the copies above, and step 0b names it with --exempt.
#
# A copy is not redundancy to clean up here: each tool decides which reader it imports. The
# verify stage checks that the installed reader resolves inside this staged tree and that the
# tree's HEAD is the commit manifest.json records for chiplet-spec. Its step 0b
# (ci/check_vendored_reader.py) holds the description's and interposer-pnr's copies and, with
# --root, gds_to_kicad's byte-equal to this tree's reference/python/chiplet_format_io, so those
# three lines above are checked at every build, not only measured. The description's copy sits
# in this tree and moves with this one ref; interposer-pnr's moves with STAGE_INTERPOSER_PNR_REF
# and gds_to_kicad's with the tools/gds_to_kicad gitlink, so both move together with this ref.
# The plugin's tests require the staged ref to be the commit its copy names, and the ADK's oracle
# resolves the commit its vendor/README.md names in the staged tree, so the
# tools/chiplet_kicad_plugin and tools/adk gitlinks move with this ref.
#
# Strict admission, the export path and the ADK's own copy all read with 1.22.0. The ADK's
# admission tests build their documents from the pin list in examples/two_die_interposer, not from
# that example's .chiplet, so nothing in them checks that document against the 1.22.0 reader, and
# this file does not assert it.
#
# Every commit the ADK and the plugin declare for their copies is this ref, the HEAD of the
# staged clone, so no extra commit is fetched. chiplet-spec's own test of its 1.2 reader runs from
# the vendored fixture above, so it needs no commit outside this tree.
STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_PATH=chiplet-spec
STAGE_CHIPLET_SPEC_REF=9da50b2c4e31074792201b139743b93c3351e18d

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# LibreLane
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# LibreLane as the die builds ran it (the cores and the DRIVER and ECHO dies): upstream
# LIBRELANE_REV with LIBRELANE_PATCHES applied in order. It is pinned by revision on its
# public upstream rather than staged from a local checkout like the STAGE_* entries, and the
# change those builds carried in LibreLane's own source is a tracked patch, so that divergence
# is visible here. LIBRELANE_PATCHES lists patch files in this repository, separated by spaces.
# The one patch touches only librelane/scripts/pyosys/ys_common.py: it accepts a Yosys whose
# Python binding is the top-level libyosys module (upstream at this revision refuses it,
# meaning to require Yosys 0.59.1 or higher), prints the no-binding error to stderr, and calls
# a pass through the binding's mangled overload name when Pass.call is absent.
#
# The Docker image does not install LibreLane; build.sh leaves it out of the image manifest for
# that reason. flake.nix reads LIBRELANE_REV and LIBRELANE_PATCHES for the Nix build shell
# (devShells.build): its librelane input is the same commit, its evaluation refuses a
# flake.lock whose librelane revision is not LIBRELANE_REV, and packages.librelane-src applies
# LIBRELANE_PATCHES to that input. The flake also gives this LibreLane a patched standalone
# OpenSTA (packages.opensta); that patch is not a LibreLane patch and is not listed here.
# Nothing reads LIBRELANE_REPO; flake.nix names the same repository in its librelane URL.
#
# A move of LIBRELANE_REV takes flake.nix's librelane URL and flake.lock with it, and every
# patch must still apply to the new revision. flake.nix's nix-eda input, which the librelane
# input follows, moves only together with LIBRELANE_REV, and NIX_OPENROAD_OUTPATH moves too
# when OpenROAD changes (see the "OpenROAD, for the Nix shell" section).
LIBRELANE_REPO=https://github.com/librelane/librelane.git
LIBRELANE_REV=359bedc8aef928648b2fa247b9e845f4ec934276
LIBRELANE_PATCHES="patches/librelane-ys_common.patch"

# The flake's patched source is a store path with no commit of its own; with --realize,
# nix/check-build-pins.sh compares it file by file with the tree `git apply` makes from
# LIBRELANE_REV and LIBRELANE_PATCHES in a local LibreLane clone, leaving out submodule paths,
# which the flake input does not carry.
#
# LIBRELANE_PATCHED_REV names a commit made from those two with LIBRELANE_PATCH_IDENT as author
# and committer and LIBRELANE_PATCH_DATE as both dates, so that a LibreLane checkout carrying
# the patch has a clean name: chiplet-flow and packaging-flow identify a checkout by
# `git rev-parse HEAD` plus `-dirty` for any uncommitted change, and a patch left uncommitted
# would read as `<sha>-dirty`. That commit's message is not in this file, so the commit cannot
# be remade from it, and nothing in this tree uses the IDENT and DATE values. The PATCHED_REV line still has
# to stay: the build shell's evaluation and the pins check each require exactly one. The flake
# passes it on in ADK_NIX_LIBRELANE_IDENTITY, and nix/capture_build_context.py records it as a
# claim without comparing its tree; the pins check compares the commit's tree only when the
# clone holds it and otherwise reports it UNVERIFIED. A run through the build shell's launcher
# is identified by the launcher's path and sha256, not by this commit.
LIBRELANE_PATCH_IDENT="adk-tools <adk-tools@localhost>"
LIBRELANE_PATCH_DATE="2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"
LIBRELANE_PATCHED_REV=3dec2a1c1882446f871785e5506523e2357e20cf

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenROAD, for the Nix shell
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The OpenROAD the adk-tools Nix cli and build shells use, as the store path it resolves to for
# x86_64-linux. It is derived, not chosen: flake.nix's packages.openroad is the `librelane`
# input's own (librelane/nix/openroad.nix, rev + sha256), built under nix-eda 6.4.0, which that
# input follows. The input is locked at the LIBRELANE_REV commit (see the "LibreLane" section), and
# there it packages OpenROAD dcf36133a369abc8f3c5e5738cd4d82e4903c0e0. nix/cli-env.sh exports
# OPENROAD as that package's bin/openroad; this line is the output path, not the binary.
#
# Two checks read this line. nix/check-openroad-pin.sh evaluates the flake's output path against
# the committed flake.lock, by evaluation alone, and refuses on any difference, so a flake.lock or
# LIBRELANE_REV move that changes OpenROAD fails there until this line moves with it; it also
# refuses anything but one double-quoted, unexported /nix/store/<hash>-openroad literal.
# nix/check-build-pins.sh checks that the patched LibreLane package's derivation graph builds
# this OpenROAD and no other derivation of that name, so LibreLane in the build shell carries this
# OpenROAD and no second build of it. That is why flake.nix hands LibreLane the patched OpenSTA
# through its package argument and not an overlay, which would rebuild OpenROAD.
#
# build.sh checks the line's shape, writes it as the runtime image's org.ihp.adk-tools.nix-openroad
# label, and passes it to the runtime stage, which carries that path in its own /nix/store (with
# the closure nix/host-closure.txt lists) and sets OPENROAD to its bin/openroad, which is where
# `interposer-pnr route` finds it. So the runtime image's layers change with this line. For an
# image built before that, run.sh reads the label from the image it launches, mounts the store the
# path is in read-only, and passes OPENROAD (ADK_OPENROAD names another). bin/adk-tools reports
# whether $OPENROAD or PATH has one.
#
# interposer-pnr states its routing as proven against one OpenROAD build, the one its
# tools_manifest.json names for routing, and drift from it only warns. The router at the staged
# interposer-pnr revision adapts to this build (its detailed-route Tcl drops
# -bottom/-top_routing_layer when detailed_route's own body rejects them, as dcf36133's does, and
# corrects dcf36133's rdl_route, which drops every net given by name), but this build is not an
# established equivalent of that one. packaging-flow's live_gate test names no build; its route leg
# runs whatever OPENROAD, else PATH, gives, as interposer-pnr's live route tests do. So a green
# live route test in either repository says nothing about this build unless the OpenROAD it found
# was this one, as it is inside the Nix shell, or in the verify stage's step 0g, which runs
# interposer-pnr's single-layer live route cases on this build. The routing build cannot be pinned
# this way: no flake that builds it is left to lock.
#
# It is not called OPENROAD on purpose: this file is sourced, and in a shell that already exports
# OPENROAD (the Nix shell does) that assignment would change what every child sees.
#
# Do not confuse this with openroad/openroad.pin, which pins OpenROAD ad9e7248 for the 3Dblox
# sidecar image, a different build for a different job. interposer-pnr's tools_manifest.json also
# keeps two OpenROAD pins by role (one for routing, ad9e7248 for 3Dblox).
NIX_OPENROAD_OUTPATH="/nix/store/ihqbw193x4qgdal58v4fczbaq1j3pc03-openroad-2026-02-17"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# LibreLane and the sg13g2 PDK, for a die built in the container
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The three store paths behind what the Nix build shell sets as ADK_NIX_LIBRELANE,
# ADK_NIX_LIBRELANE_PYTHON and ADK_NIX_SG13G2_PDK for x86_64-linux: flake.nix's
# packages.librelane, the patched LibreLane of the "LibreLane" section, whose derivation builds the
# OpenROAD above; packages.librelane-python, the Python env whose own site-packages holds that
# LibreLane, which core preparation runs as its interpreter; and packages.sg13g2-pdk, the PDK tree
# nix/sg13g2-pdk.nix assembles at the Dockerfile's SG13G2_REF with pdk-source.json at its root.
# Like NIX_OPENROAD_OUTPATH they are derived, not chosen, and move with flake.lock or with the
# pins those packages read.
#
# nix/check-openroad-pin.sh holds all three to the flake's evaluation as it holds
# NIX_OPENROAD_OUTPATH, and refuses anything but one double-quoted, unexported /nix/store literal
# of the package's name. build.sh checks only that shape and writes them as the runtime image's
# org.ihp.adk-tools.nix-librelane, org.ihp.adk-tools.nix-librelane-python and
# org.ihp.adk-tools.nix-sg13g2-pdk labels, and the runtime image carries each in its own
# /nix/store and sets it in its ENV under the build shell's variable name. The interpreter's label
# is the env's bin/python3.<minor>, the file the build shell's ADK_NIX_LIBRELANE_PYTHON names
# (its bin/python3 is a link, which core preparation refuses). For an image built before that,
# run.sh passes each into the container under that name when the path is on the host, in the
# store it mounts for OpenROAD; otherwise it says so and starts anyway, and a page that builds a
# die refuses.
NIX_LIBRELANE_OUTPATH="/nix/store/8bj13r4a4wwmsdgpf47z5v93fhm5jyjl-python3.13-librelane-3.0.0rc0"
NIX_LIBRELANE_PYTHON_OUTPATH="/nix/store/jys2hxxpx11x2nbv3rvmhaks224nixmq-python3-3.13.9-env"
NIX_SG13G2_PDK_OUTPATH="/nix/store/hx96lf7n4m0if0nl48m5ydj90rvga8z2-ihp-open-pdk-22f4335"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Nix lane's interpreter, not in the image
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# flake.nix's packages.python, the CLI shell's cli-env: the interpreter with the third-party
# Python the flow imports, whose site-packages the shell sets as ADK_NIX_SITE_PACKAGES. Derived,
# not chosen, like the pins above: it moves with flake.lock or with cli-env's package list.
#
# nix/check-openroad-pin.sh holds it to the flake's evaluation as it holds the others. The image
# runs its own venv's interpreter, never this path, so nix/tool_identity.py leaves this pin out of
# the image's facts and table and the image's python row names its own; the lane's table keeps
# this one as its python row. build.sh neither checks it nor passes it on.
NIX_PYTHON_OUTPATH="/nix/store/w2333a98nw88y34dgxlnh7wx7k206zq3-python3-3.13.9-env"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Co-simulation engine, shipped in the image
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# chiplet-cosim admits a gate-level run only when its capability table
# (cosim_engine_capability.json) holds an entry for the exact engine: the iverilog, vvp and cocotb
# VPI library sha256 and the cocotb version, among the rest of its key. Its entry for sg13g2 cells
# under Icarus was shown on the Icarus and the cocotb these store paths hold, so the image ships
# exactly them: flake.nix's packages.cosim-iverilog, packages.cosim-cocotb (cocotb 2.0.0, which is
# not nix-eda's default cocotb) and packages.cosim-python, a python3.13 with cocotb in its own
# site-packages, where the harness check looks for it. Another Icarus or cocotb is another engine
# that no entry covers, so none of these lines moves before chiplet-cosim's table has an entry for
# what it moves to.
#
# The devshell chiplet-cosim reads from COSIM_DEVSHELL is a store path of its own,
# packages.cosim-devshell: a bin/ of exactly three links, python3 to the env and iverilog and vvp
# to Icarus. A run's lock pins the devshell by that path, so the host and the image carry the same
# one and a lock replays in either. It moves whenever the env or Icarus moves.
#
# nix/check-openroad-pin.sh holds the four to the flake's evaluation as it holds the pins above.
# Unlike those, these go into the image. build.sh checks nix/cosim-closure.txt against the host
# store's own closure of the devshell, the python env and Icarus (a store query; nothing evaluates
# Nix), so the paths have to be on the host: nix build .#cosim-devshell. It then packs the listed
# paths into cosim-closure.tar in the build context (generated, not tracked) and passes the four
# paths as build arguments. The runtime stage unpacks the closure under /nix/store and links
# /opt/adk-tools/cosim-devshell, which COSIM_DEVSHELL names, to the devshell's store path. The
# verify stage's last leg checks that link and the engine against the entry.
NIX_COSIM_IVERILOG_OUTPATH="/nix/store/nknbcs56x54kqhbv0slfca6v4gmi724v-iverilog-s20250103-60-gdb82380ce"
NIX_COSIM_COCOTB_OUTPATH="/nix/store/yk336mn39lfjiawz9zq4qbgq29vhd694-python3.13-cocotb-2.0.0"
NIX_COSIM_PYTHON_OUTPATH="/nix/store/1czlxfa90k7h85aq923imkr9x2fjmz5v-python3-3.13.9-env"
NIX_COSIM_DEVSHELL_OUTPATH="/nix/store/kcxwxk9fj86r0l2ih49hk3gihk6la9sk-cosim-devshell"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mixed-signal engine, shipped in the image
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The engine for an analog netlist under ngspice with digital parts under Icarus, apart from the
# co-simulation one above: flake.nix's packages.mixsim-ngspice (nixpkgs' ngspice 45, built with
# XSPICE and OSDI, without KLU), packages.mixsim-iverilog (an Icarus with libvvp, at the same rev
# as the co-simulation Icarus) and packages.sg13g2-osdi, the six sg13g2 OSDI models compiled for
# generic x86-64 from the Verilog-A sources at SG13G2_REF. ngspice's d_cosim runs Icarus through
# the ivlng shim in ngspice's lib/ngspice, given libvvp and the VPI module by absolute path, so no
# loader path search is involved (the flake's checks.mixsim-explicit-paths shows it).
#
# The OSDI files are not reproducible from their recipe by every compiler run, so their identity
# is their bytes: nix/sg13g2-osdi.sha256 names the six files' sha256, and build.sh refuses unless
# the store path below holds exactly them. A rebuild of that path with other bytes is another
# engine, and the file and this line move together.
#
# nix/check-openroad-pin.sh holds the three to the flake's evaluation like the pins above, and
# like the co-simulation engine they go into the image: build.sh checks nix/mixsim-closure.txt
# against the host store's closure of the three, packs it into mixsim-closure.tar, and passes the
# three paths as build arguments. The runtime stage unpacks it under /nix/store and sets
# MIXSIM_DEVSHELL to /opt/adk-tools/mixsim-devshell: bin/ngspice, bin/iverilog and bin/vvp,
# lib/libvvp.so, lib/ngspice (ngspice's own, with the ivlng shim and VPI module and the code
# models) and osdi/, each a link into these paths. The verify stage's mixed-signal leg checks that
# directory, the six files' sha256 and both engines live.
NIX_MIXSIM_NGSPICE_OUTPATH="/nix/store/mjipfrxz15vc3ls2m9yiy0zw987vp38m-ngspice-45"
NIX_MIXSIM_IVERILOG_OUTPATH="/nix/store/r95n1sqiyfmpnw4jy3znxb91a8khy84g-iverilog-libvvp-s20250103-60-gdb82380ce"
NIX_SG13G2_OSDI_OUTPATH="/nix/store/anybmg3w0nlzrl57xms9r9hpai93x4sl-sg13g2-osdi-22f4335"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Waveform viewers, shipped in the image
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# What a run's waveforms are opened with: flake.nix's packages.gtkwave (GTKWave 3.3.121, for VCD
# and FST, with vcd2fst and fst2vcd) and packages.gaw (GAW, which reads ngspice's raw files, binary
# and ASCII), both nixpkgs' at the plain nixpkgs the lock names, so the binary cache's store paths;
# and packages.xschem (Xschem, which draws a mixed run's analog waves from a sheet that loads its
# raw file): nixpkgs' expression with the source at master 8d2088ed, unpatched, built here (not in
# the binary cache). `xschem -v` prints "XSCHEM V3.4.8RC"; share/xschem/xschem_library/devices is
# the source tree's, byte for byte. stdenv rewrote its awk scripts' #! lines to Nix's gawk, so
# gawk joins the viewers' closure.
#
# nix/check-openroad-pin.sh holds the three to the flake's evaluation like the pins above, and
# like the engines they go into the image: build.sh checks nix/viewer-closure.txt against the host
# store's closure of the three, less what the engines' lists already hold, packs it into
# viewer-closure.tar, and passes the three paths as build arguments. The tools stage unpacks it
# under /nix/store and links /usr/local/bin/gtkwave, /usr/local/bin/gaw and /usr/local/bin/xschem
# into these paths, so all three are on PATH, and sets XSCHEM_SHAREDIR to Xschem's share/xschem.
# The verify stage's viewer leg checks the links and opens a waveform in each, headless.
NIX_GTKWAVE_OUTPATH="/nix/store/8kygk1ymkq6dqym0xv6hy08bxw3px6dv-gtkwave-3.3.121"
NIX_GAW_OUTPATH="/nix/store/7gjgfdhglwb682gdv0qmqqxsskkdqlh4-gaw3-20250128"
NIX_XSCHEM_OUTPATH="/nix/store/af01dvh78hfmxljwq4v4lm56apxp25hh-xschem-3.4.8RC-8d2088e"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verilog-AMS engine, shipped in the image
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# What runs a Verilog-AMS deck: flake.nix's packages.vams-gnucap, gnucap at develop b6678fe
# (version lines trace_adv 2026.09.25) and gnucap-modelgen-verilog at develop 100e746, both from
# savannah, built into one prefix by the plain nixpkgs the lock names, so gnucap finds the model
# generator's plugins on its own plugin path and libgnucap.so by its rpath. packages.vams-devshell
# is VAMS_DEVSHELL: a bin/ of gnucap, gnucap-conf, gnucap-mg-vams and gnucap-modelgen, and g++ and
# c++, the stdenv's own GCC, which builds a run's modelgen output against the same libstdc++.
#
# nix/check-openroad-pin.sh holds the two to the flake's evaluation like the pins above. Neither is
# in the binary cache: the first image build after a change here needs both built on the host
# (nix build .#vams-devshell). build.sh checks nix/vams-closure.txt against the host store's closure
# of the devshell, less what the engines' and viewers' lists already hold, packs it into
# vams-closure.tar, and passes the two paths as build arguments. The runtime stage unpacks it under
# /nix/store and links /opt/adk-tools/vams-devshell to the devshell; nothing goes on PATH. The
# verify stage's Verilog-AMS leg checks the links, then builds a model with the devshell's g++ and
# solves a deck with it.
NIX_VAMS_GNUCAP_OUTPATH="/nix/store/yawvmxraq2bapnsapsb5c2j6j0c856b4-gnucap-b6678fe-modelgen-verilog-100e746"
NIX_VAMS_DEVSHELL_OUTPATH="/nix/store/1dlfq2bbq2db4dc3bx897zxacf7jbkga-vams-devshell"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Die views, shipped in the image
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The EDA views of six built dies, as chiplet-flow's emit-eda-views wrote them: the two sg13g2
# bondpad dies (CF_DRIVER_BONDPAD and CF_ECHO_BONDPAD), CF_DRIVER_LS (the DRIVER rebuilt with level
# shifters at its pads), the gf180mcu CF_ECHO_CX_BONDPAD, and the sigma-delta pair: SD_CORE_BONDPAD
# on gf180mcuD and SD_AFE_BONDPAD on sg13g2. Their gate-level netlists and simulation models are
# among them, and CDL where the record has it (CF_ECHO_CX_BONDPAD's lists cdl under not_recorded).
# One directory per die,
# named by the layout sha256 its contract binds, holding the record (eda-views.json) and every
# file it names. They come from die runs, not from a repository, so there is no commit to stage.
# die-views.sha256 lists every file by its sha256 under the emitted paths
# (records/<layout sha256>/<file>), and the line below names that listing by its own sha256: the
# recipe commit pins every byte, and a listing that moves without this line refuses.
#
# Where the records sit on the building host is not part of the recipe. stage-die-views.sh, which
# build.sh runs after stage-sources.sh, takes the directory from STAGE_DIE_VIEWS_DIR in the
# environment, absolute, with no default, checks it read-only against the listing, copies the
# listed files into die-views/ in the build context (generated, not tracked) and checks the copy.
# So the image is rebuildable where the records exist, which is the same reduction as staging and
# for the same reason. The runtime stage puts them under /opt/adk-tools/die-views/<layout
# sha256>/, checks them there against the listing and the manifest's die_views entry, and sets
# ADK_DIE_VIEWS to that directory. A die whose layout sha256 has no directory there has no views.
STAGE_DIE_VIEWS_SHA256="8b080b8dc5e075f68f7a9a83b7cf16978267fde28936e35315798b9ed2abddd2"
